  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 116: Cooking with your toddler: what kitchen involvement can do for their eating — and how to keep it safe
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   116,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Cooking with your toddler: what kitchen involvement can do for their eating — and how to keep it safe',
   '"Small hands in the kitchen build curiosity about food — not because cooking is a shortcut, but because it''s contact, without any pressure attached."',
   'Involving children in cooking may help build food-preparation skills, confidence and positive engagement with food, but the evidence is mixed and comes mainly from structured programmes involving preschool-aged or school-aged children. Some studies report improvements in willingness to try foods, food attitudes or self-regulation, while changes in actual dietary intake are less consistent. Evidence specifically isolating ordinary kitchen helping in two-year-olds is limited. Kitchen involvement can therefore be worthwhile for connection, learning and low-pressure familiarity, without presenting it as a proven way to prevent or resolve selective eating. Because a working kitchen also contains heat, sharp objects, raw ingredients and trip hazards, the activity must be separated clearly from active hot cooking. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. The medical-condition notes below apply to the mother. They are not a treatment plan for the child.',
   'If an elder in your household believes a toddler should be doing more in the kitchen than feels safe to you: you can gently hold the line that the same principle applies regardless of who''s teaching: knives and other sharp cutting tools, and stove proximity, wait for the hand-eye coordination and impulse control this age genuinely doesn''t have yet — that''s not a reflection on your toddler or your parenting, and it isn''t a rule you invented. If your toddler has already been burned or scalded, climbs onto counters or stools without warning, or your kitchen layout makes it hard to keep hot surfaces well out of reach, it''s entirely reasonable to keep kitchen involvement to moments when the stove and any hot liquid are completely off, rather than during active cooking. There''s no required timeline for when a toddler should start helping — it depends on your own kitchen and your own child. If your toddler is burned or scalded, move them away from the heat source and cool the affected area under cool running water for 20 minutes. Remove nearby clothing, jewellery and the nappy if they are not stuck to the skin. Do not use ice, very cold water, butter, toothpaste, creams or other home remedies, and do not burst blisters. After cooling, cover the area loosely with clean cling film laid over the burn or a sterile, non-fluffy, non-stick dressing; do not wrap it tightly around a limb. Keep the rest of the child warm. Obtain prompt medical advice for a burn in a child under five whenever you are uncertain what to do. Seek emergency care for burns that are large, deep, white or charred; affect the face, airway, hands, feet, major joints, genitals or bottom; circle a limb; or are caused by chemicals or electricity. Never leave your toddler alone in the kitchen while the stove is on, a pressure cooker is in use, or hot liquid is within reach — not even for a few seconds to answer the door or a phone call. If you must step away, take your toddler with you or turn off the heat first. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this requires turning your kitchen into a classroom, or a hazard zone to avoid. A toddler can build real comfort with food and real small skills through short, safe, low-pressure moments — the safety habits above are simply what let you say yes to those moments without worry. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————- Meat, fish and eggs have no special relevance to this week''s theme and remain entirely optional. Choose whichever meal suits your family. This week''s guidance is about safe, age-appropriate kitchen involvement, not about restricting any particular meal or ingredient. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————- Separate kitchen time into two modes. During active hot cooking, the toddler stays outside a clearly defined safety zone and away from the adult''s walking path. During toddler helping time, burners, hot appliances and pressure cookers are off; hot food and liquids have been moved out of reach; and only the ingredients and utensils needed for the activity are available.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "What actually happens when your toddler helps", "notes": "Studies of children''s cooking programmes suggest possible improvements in cooking skills, confidence, food attitudes and willingness to explore foods, although results vary and most participants have been older than the toddler in this plan. Evidence for changes in actual food intake is less consistent. A Norwegian trial that taught parents to prepare homemade food found no later difference in their toddlers'' fruit and vegetable intake or food wariness, but the toddlers themselves were not the cooking participants. Other small toddler programmes have combined food preparation with responsive parenting or self-regulation activities, making it difficult to isolate the effect of cooking alone. Kitchen involvement is therefore best offered as an enjoyable learning experience — not as a guaranteed treatment for selective eating.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable appam with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable appam with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with chana dal and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe papaya, deseeded and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft hummus thinly spread on soft roti strips", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Kitchen tasks your toddler can actually manage", "notes": "At two to three years old, a toddler''s hands and attention span suit simple, repetitive tasks rather than anything requiring precision or strength — and every task should happen at floor level or on a stable, purpose-designed platform under your direct supervision, never on an ordinary chair, stool or countertop. Washing produce in a shallow bowl, tearing greens or soft herbs, snapping the ends off beans, stirring an already-mixed cool batter in a stable bowl, pouring a cool, pre-measured ingredient from a small cup, and shaking a securely fastened closed container of something like dry lentils are all realistic at this age. Collect torn pieces and bean ends as you go, rather than leaving them within easy reach of a curious mouth. Knives, peelers, graters, skewers, electrical appliances and any hot cookware remain adult-only, regardless of how capable your toddler seems in the moment — these belong to a later stage of development. Stay within arm''s reach throughout, and keep sessions short: five or ten focused minutes is often plenty before attention wanders, and that''s a feature of this age, not a problem to solve.", "breakfast_a": "Bajra chilla with grated carrot and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Bajra chilla with grated carrot and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with masoor dal and finely chopped spinach", "lunch_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with masoor dal and fully cooked egg stirred through", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe custard apple (sitaphal), seeds removed and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Steamed and mashed sweet potato with roasted peanut powder, finely ground, stirred through, if peanut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a chana dal and soft-cooked bhindi (ladies finger), finely chopped and cooked until no longer sticky", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with a chana dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked bhindi (ladies finger), finely chopped and cooked until no longer sticky If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Preventing burns and scalds", "notes": "For toddlers, hot drinks, steam, pans, cooker contents, hot surfaces and open flames can all cause serious injury. Create a clearly marked safety zone around the stove and any appliance or surface holding something hot; an adult arm''s length is not a reliable barrier for a fast-moving child. Keep pot handles turned away from the edge, use back burners where possible and keep appliance cords, hot drinks and serving dishes beyond reach. Never hold or carry a toddler while cooking or while carrying hot food or liquid. Release pressure from a pressure cooker only according to the manufacturer''s instructions and only when the toddler is outside the cooking area. If the adult must move a hot vessel, the toddler should first be placed with another responsible adult or in a secure location away from the carrying route.", "breakfast_a": "Idiyappam (string hoppers) with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Idiyappam (string hoppers) with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with moong dal and soft-cooked zucchini (tori), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with moong dal, finely crumbled paneer and soft-cooked zucchini (tori), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe fig (anjeer), stem removed and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted makhana, finely crushed and softened for the toddler, with mashed banana stirred through", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked raw banana (kancha kela), finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked raw banana (kancha kela), finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Keeping hands, mouths and hygiene safe while they help", "notes": "Give the toddler only ingredients that are safe to touch and taste in their uncooked state. Keep raw meat, poultry, fish and eggs completely separate from the toddler''s activity area. Do not let them taste raw batter or dough containing uncooked flour or egg. Wash both the adult''s and toddler''s hands with soap and running water before food preparation, after touching raw animal foods, after using the toilet or changing a nappy, and whenever hands become contaminated. Wash produce under safe running water; do not use soap or household detergent on food. Clean the work surface and utensils before and after the activity. Raw vegetables and fruit they''re helping to wash or tear are generally fine to nibble, but the same shape-and-size rules that already guide your table still apply: whole grapes must be quartered, hard raw vegetable pieces should be very finely cut or avoided raw, and anything round, hard or slippery stays off the tasting list regardless of how excited they are to try what they made.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat and vegetable uttapam with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Wheat and vegetable uttapam with finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Mini idli with mild vegetable sambar", "lunch_b": "Mini idli with mild vegetable sambar and boneless shredded chicken", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe muskmelon, deseeded and cut into small, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground almond mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if almond has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and soft-cooked ash gourd (petha), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked ash gourd (petha), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When ''help me cook'' turns into pressure to eat", "notes": "It''s tempting to use a toddler''s pride in what they''ve made as leverage — ''you helped make it, so you have to eat it'' — but this risks the same backfire seen with any pressure to eat: children who are pressured tend to eat less, not more, and react to the food more negatively, not less. There''s no direct research showing that cooking involvement itself causes this problem, but the logic carries over from what''s well established about pressure generally. The more useful approach is to let your toddler''s role stay exactly what it was — helping, out of curiosity and enjoyment — and treat what happens at the table as a separate, pressure-free decision, the same way you would with any other meal. If they don''t want to taste what they made, that''s fine; the value of the activity doesn''t depend on the outcome at the table. If mealtime pressure is already a pattern in your house, that''s an extremely common place to land — not a sign you''ve done something wrong, and one small shift is enough to start with.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain dosa with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Multigrain dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft paneer and vegetable pulao", "lunch_b": "Soft tofu and vegetable pulao, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe apple, peeled, cored and finely grated", "nourishment_break_b": "Mashed avocado and chickpea spread on soft toast fingers", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked raw jackfruit (kathal), finely chopped and cooked until soft", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked raw jackfruit (kathal), finely chopped and cooked until soft If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Everyday kitchen presence, not a special production", "notes": "Many Indian households don''t need a scheduled ''cooking activity'' to give a toddler kitchen exposure — an elder or parent is often cooking daily in a shared or nearby space, with the toddler simply present. That everyday presence — watching, smelling, occasionally being handed something to hold or stir — counts too, and may be a more realistic and sustainable form of involvement than a dedicated session every week. The broader idea that repeated, low-pressure exposure to a food can make it feel more familiar over time is reasonably well supported for tasting exposure specifically; whether the same holds for watching or helping prepare a food without eating it is plausible, but hasn''t been directly tested in toddlers. Either way, there''s no need to formalize this into a project: a toddler can still be included through brief, planned moments — smelling an ingredient, washing produce at a safe station or stirring a cool mixture — without standing in the cooking or carrying path.", "breakfast_a": "Broken wheat (dalia) porridge with grated carrot and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Broken wheat (dalia) porridge with grated carrot and soft tofu, finely crumbled, mixed through, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe pear, peeled, cored and finely grated or stewed until soft", "nourishment_break_b": "Stewed apple with oats and cinnamon, without added sugar", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal and soft-cooked raw papaya (kancha papita), finely grated and cooked until soft", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal and finely crumbled paneer, with soft-cooked raw papaya (kancha papita), finely grated and cooked until soft If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Picking one task to try this week", "notes": "This week, choose one short task — such as tearing washed greens at the table, pouring a pre-measured cool ingredient into a stable bowl or stirring a cool, already-mixed batter. Set up the activity before the stove is switched on or after the cooking area has completely cooled. Stay within immediate reach, and stop when your toddler''s attention moves elsewhere.", "breakfast_a": "Palak (spinach) and besan cheela with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Palak (spinach) and besan cheela with finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Soft sweet-potato and moong dal patties with cooked capsicum", "lunch_b": "Soft sweet-potato and moong dal patties with fully cooked fish, carefully deboned and flaked", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe strawberries, hulled and quartered", "nourishment_break_b": "Steamed and mashed green peas with a pinch of roasted cumin, mixed into a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely diced", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mixed dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely diced If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. What''s one small task your toddler could realistically join in on this week, given your own kitchen and schedule — and what would need to be true for it to feel safe and manageable rather than stressful?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'A toddler in the kitchen isn''t a proven shortcut to better eating, but it can be a genuinely good use of a few short, low-pressure minutes together — building real skills and a calmer relationship with food, at their own pace and within safety limits that make sense for your own home. Some weeks that may be a planned activity; on others, it may be one short task completed at a safe station before hot cooking begins.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year. This week''s lesson can travel forward with you: involvement works best when it is brief, pressure-free and built around boundaries that keep curiosity safely away from preventable harm.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 117: Eating safely through the monsoon: food, water and appetite for your toddler
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   117,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Eating safely through the monsoon: food, water and appetite for your toddler',
   '"Monsoon doesn''t call for a new set of rules — it calls for the ones you already have, held a little more consistently until the rains ease."',
   'Monsoon genuinely raises a toddler''s risk of diarrhoeal illness in India — this isn''t just folk caution. Contaminated water, flooding and sewage overflow drive real, documented spikes in cholera and other diarrhoeal disease during the rainy months, and young children carry a disproportionate share of that burden. The response is not a long new list of monsoon-only rules. It is the same water-safety, handwashing, cooking and storage habits you already know, followed more consistently—particularly when handling leafy vegetables, cut produce and dry staples exposed to damp conditions. A few other common monsoon beliefs, like cut fruit being risky because of humidity itself, or particular foods ''boosting immunity,'' are less well supported than they''re often presented as, and this week tries to be honest about which is which. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. The medical-condition notes below apply to the mother. They are not a treatment plan for the child.',
   'If an elder in your household insists a specific food or drink — turmeric milk, a particular herb, a special tonic — is essential to protect your toddler from monsoon illness: you can gently explain that no single food or drink can prevent monsoon infections — a varied, adequate diet supports normal immune function, but tonics and herbs don''t replace safe water, vaccination, hygiene, sufficient nourishment or medical care. There''s no harm including a food an elder values as part of an ordinary diet — the concern is only if it replaces those habits, or is pushed on a toddler who doesn''t want it. If your toddler has had recent diarrhoeal illness, is on antibiotics, is underweight, or you know your area''s water supply has been affected by flooding this season, it''s worth being especially strict about boiled water and freshly cooked food, and lowering your threshold for calling your paediatrician if symptoms appear. If your toddler develops diarrhoea or vomiting, continue feeding and offer oral rehydration solution (ORS) in small, frequent sips alongside their usual food and milk. Packet ORS prepared with exactly the stated amount of safe water is preferred — do not make it stronger or weaker, add extra sugar or salt, or mix it with milk or juice. If ORS is temporarily unavailable, continue breastfeeding and suitable fluids while promptly asking a paediatrician, pharmacist or local health service what to use until you can obtain it. Continue feeding and resume normal food as soon as your toddler is willing, rather than withholding it. Zinc is also commonly recommended for children with diarrhoea, typically for 10 to 14 days — ask your paediatrician or pharmacist for the correct age-appropriate product, dose and duration rather than using a routine multivitamin or guessing the amount. Seek medical care immediately if your toddler is unusually sleepy or difficult to wake, has sunken eyes, is unwilling or unable to drink, is passing little or no urine, has cool or discoloured hands and feet, has blood in their stool, or has a high fever alongside persistent vomiting that prevents any fluid intake. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this is meant to make monsoon feel like a hazard to get through. Real, documented risk exists in India during these months, but it''s addressed with the everyday water and food habits you already have — held a little more consistently until the rains ease. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————– Meat, fish and eggs have no special relevance to this week''s theme and remain entirely optional. Choose whichever meal suits your family. Wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully where required, whichever option you choose. ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————–',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Why waterborne illness risk rises during monsoon", "notes": "Heavy rain and flooding can allow sewage and other contamination to enter drinking-water systems. Studies from different parts of India have found seasonal increases in diarrhoeal illness after heavy rainfall, although the level of risk varies by location, water source and sanitation conditions. The practical response is not fear — it is following local water advisories and being especially consistent about safe water, handwashing, food preparation and storage.", "breakfast_a": "Jowar dosa with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Jowar dosa with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with chana dal and soft-cooked cauliflower (phool gobi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with chana dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cauliflower (phool gobi), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe guava (amrud), deseeded and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Steamed and mashed carrot with a pinch of roasted cumin, mixed into a small bowl of warm milk", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Water safety: the same rules, applied more consistently", "notes": "If your local authority advises boiling water, do so even if the water has passed through an ordinary household filter. Different filters remove different contaminants, and many portable filters do not reliably remove viruses or bacteria — follow the filter manufacturer''s verified claims and local public-health advice rather than assuming a filter alone is equivalent to boiling. A rolling boil for a full minute reliably kills the organisms behind most waterborne illness at most elevations; above roughly 2,000 metres, boil for three minutes instead. Boiling does not remove chemical contaminants. Research also shows stored water quality often drops after it''s already been made safe, so keep boiled or filtered water in a clean, covered container and use a clean ladle or narrow-spouted container rather than dipping hands or cups in — this matters as much for water used to rinse fruit or prepare food as for drinking water itself.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal dosa with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal dosa with a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft idli with a mild sambar, made with masoor dal and finely chopped amaranth leaves (chaulai saag), cooked thoroughly", "lunch_b": "Soft idli with a mild sambar, made with masoor dal and finely chopped amaranth leaves (chaulai saag), cooked thoroughly, with a fully cooked egg curry alongside", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe kiwi, peeled and finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft paneer and grated cucumber, mixed with a squeeze of lime", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a chana dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with a chana dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Cut fruit, salads and street food — what''s actually riskier", "notes": "The common caution about cut fruit and street food during monsoon is directionally reasonable, but the usual explanation — that humidity itself makes bacteria grow faster on the fruit — is more complicated than that. What genuinely rises during monsoon is the likelihood that the water used to wash or prepare food, or the surface it''s cut on, carries contamination. This isn''t a reason for a blanket ban on eating outside — it''s a way to choose lower-risk options. When eating outside, prefer food that is cooked thoroughly and served piping hot. Be cautious with cut fruit, raw chutneys, salads, ice and anything that''s been sitting at room temperature, when you can''t assess the water source, refrigeration or handling behind it. At home, the safer choice is fruit you''ve washed and cut yourself, with water you trust.", "breakfast_a": "Rava uttapam with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Rava uttapam with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with moong dal and soft-cooked green beans (fansi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with moong dal, finely crumbled paneer and soft-cooked green beans (fansi), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe orange flesh, peel, seeds and tough membrane removed, finely chopped into manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Steamed and mashed sweet potato with a pinch of cinnamon and roasted chana (chickpea) flour stirred through", "dinner_a": "Soft appam with a mild vegetable stew containing soft-cooked potato, carrot and green peas (mataar), gently mashed or finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft appam with a mild, fully cooked chicken stew containing boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked potato and carrot If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Washing and cooking leafy greens safely", "notes": "Leafy greens don''t need to be feared or avoided during monsoon — they need a bit more care in how they''re washed and cooked. Leafy vegetables grow close to the ground and are often irrigated with water that can carry soil- and water-borne contamination; documented cases in India include children infected with an intestinal parasite traced to vegetables irrigated with contaminated water. Separate and discard any damaged or visibly soiled leaves, then wash the rest thoroughly with reliably safe running water, rubbing and separating the leaves so every surface is reached — salt water is not required and isn''t a dependable way to disinfect vegetables. Cook leafy greens thoroughly when serving them to your toddler. Thorough washing helps reduce surface contamination, while thorough cooking provides additional protection against many disease-causing organisms. Both steps matter; neither makes visibly spoiled or chemically contaminated produce safe.", "breakfast_a": "Oats and vegetable chilla with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Oats and vegetable chilla with finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Rice with a mixed dal and soft-cooked drumstick leaves (moringa saag), finely chopped and cooked thoroughly", "lunch_b": "Rice with a mixed dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked drumstick leaves (moringa saag), finely chopped and cooked thoroughly", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe dragon fruit (kamalam), mashed, with finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground walnut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if walnut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Keeping grains, pulses and flour safe from monsoon damp", "notes": "Warm, damp storage encourages mould growth in the grains, pulses and flours already in your kitchen, and some moulds can produce mycotoxins such as aflatoxins — linked to liver damage and, with repeated exposure, to poor growth in young children. Store only fully dry staples: grain, pulses, flour, nuts and spices need to be completely dry before they go into a clean, tightly closed, airtight container, not just sealed up damp. Buy quantities your household can use within a reasonable time rather than stockpiling. Discard anything damp, visibly mouldy, unusually discoloured, insect-damaged or musty — and don''t rely on cooking to make mould-damaged food safe, since many mycotoxins survive ordinary cooking heat. The absence of visible mould or smell can''t guarantee food is safe either, so buy staples from a reliable source and take a local recall or advisory seriously if one is issued.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat vermicelli (semiya) upma with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Wheat vermicelli (semiya) upma with finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with an arhar dal and soft-cooked yam (suran), cooked until completely soft and mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with an arhar dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked yam (suran), cooked until completely soft and mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe plum, peeled, stone removed and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Boiled and mashed sweet corn kernels, mixed into a small bowl of curd", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked potato (aloo), finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked potato (aloo), finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Appetite dips, comfort food, and ''immunity'' foods", "notes": "A toddler''s appetite can change from day to day, and can vary with activity, routine, sleep, teething or illness — rainy weather doesn''t require a special appetite remedy, and there isn''t strong evidence pointing to one particular monsoon-specific cause. Continue offering regular, varied meals without pressure, the same way you would in any other season. If reduced intake comes together with lethargy, repeated vomiting, diarrhoea, dehydration, weight loss or an ongoing change in growth, that combination is worth raising with your paediatrician rather than waiting it out. On immunity: no single food or drink can prevent monsoon infections. A varied, adequate diet supports normal immune function, but turmeric milk, herbs and tonics don''t replace safe water, vaccination, hygiene, sufficient nourishment or medical care.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi dosa with soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped and cooked thoroughly", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, finely crumbled paneer and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped and cooked thoroughly", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe jamun (Indian blackberry), stone removed and checked carefully for fragments of stone before mashing", "nourishment_break_b": "Mashed banana with roasted peanut powder, finely ground, stirred through, if peanut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "A simple monsoon routine, not a list of fears", "notes": "This week''s guidance can feel like a lot at once, so the short version is: boil or reliably treat drinking water and store it covered; wash produce thoroughly and lean toward cooked greens; wash and cut fruit yourself; check your grain and flour for dampness or must; and don''t feel pressured to add a special ''immunity'' food — a varied diet your toddler actually eats is doing the real work. None of this needs new equipment or a special monsoon shopping list, just more consistency with habits you likely already have.", "breakfast_a": "Besan cheela with grated bottle gourd (lauki) and a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Besan cheela with grated bottle gourd (lauki) and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked bhindi (ladies finger), finely chopped and cooked until no longer sticky", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked bhindi (ladies finger), finely chopped and cooked until no longer sticky", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe mulberries (shehtoot), stems removed and lightly crushed", "nourishment_break_b": "Steamed and mashed pumpkin with a pinch of roasted cumin, mixed into a small bowl of warm milk", "dinner_a": "Soft dosa with a mild mixed-vegetable sambar, made with a mixed dal, cooked until soft, portion adapted as needed", "dinner_b": "Soft dosa with a mild, fully cooked chicken-and-vegetable stew, made with boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked mixed vegetables If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Which one of this week''s habits — water storage, produce washing, or checking your pantry for damp grain — is most worth tightening up in your own kitchen this monsoon?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Monsoon brings genuine, documented risk to a toddler''s food and water in India — that deserves to be taken seriously, not brushed off. But taking it seriously looks like consistent, ordinary habits: safe water, thorough washing, cooked greens, dry storage, and calling for help early if illness happens — not a long list of foods to fear or a special tonic to add.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: separating genuine, evidenced risk from inherited habit, so your attention goes to what actually protects your toddler.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 118: Packing your toddler's tiffin: building a simple lunch and keeping it safely cold or hot
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   118,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Packing your toddler''s tiffin: building a simple lunch and keeping it safely cold or hot',
   '"A tiffin''s safety comes from real temperature control or genuinely shelf-stable food, not from how dry it looks — and it doesn''t need to be elaborate or come back empty to be doing its job."',
   'A tiffin is a different food-safety situation from a meal eaten at home, and it''s worth treating that way — not with anxiety, but with one governing rule. Cooked dal, rice, vegetables, egg, meat, fish, paneer, tofu, curd, milk and cut fruit are all perishable, however dry or simple they look, and perishable food needs to be kept reliably cold (below about 4°C), kept reliably hot (above about 60°C), refrigerated promptly on arrival, or eaten within the standard safe-time limit — two hours, or one hour once it''s hotter than 32°C. A single small ice pack often isn''t enough on its own; two adequately frozen cold sources, or a proper preheated hot-food container, do the job more reliably. The rest of this week is about scale: a packed lunch doesn''t need to be nutritionally complete on its own, doesn''t need to come back empty, and doesn''t need to look a particular way. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. The medical-condition notes below apply to the mother. They are not a treatment plan for the child.',
   'If someone feels your toddler''s tiffin looks plain, less festive, or less elaborate than other children''s: you can gently explain that a lunchbox''s job is to be safe, reasonably balanced and something your toddler will actually eat — not to look a particular way. There''s no harm making it more colourful or fun if that''s easy for you, but it isn''t necessary, and isn''t worth the extra effort if it isn''t enjoyable. If your toddler''s daycare has no refrigeration or way to reheat food, pack perishable food with two adequately frozen cold sources in an insulated bag rather than relying on dry texture or a single small ice pack, or send it piping hot in a preheated insulated food container instead — either pathway works, but ''dry'' or ''one ice pack'' on their own often don''t. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn packing a lunchbox into a research project. A few consistent habits — real cold or real heat, careful cutting, and reasonable expectations about what comes back — cover what matters. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————- Meat, fish, eggs and dairy have no special relevance to whether a meal is nutritious this week — but if any dish is going into a tiffin rather than eaten fresh at home, treat the cooked dal, vegetables, egg, meat, fish, paneer, tofu, curd, milk, fresh chutney and cut fruit in it as perishable, and keep them reliably cold or reliably hot. Choose whichever suits your family for meals eaten at home; wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully either way. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————-',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Why a lunchbox is a different food-safety situation than a home meal", "notes": "A home meal is eaten within minutes of being cooked, at a safe hot or cold temperature. A packed lunch is different: it usually sits at ambient temperature for three to five hours between packing and eating, long enough to matter. Food-safety authorities define a ''danger zone,'' roughly 4°C to 60°C, where bacteria multiply fastest, and the standard rule is that perishable food shouldn''t sit in that zone for more than two hours — or one hour once the surrounding temperature is above 32°C. Reliable cold or hot holding avoids having to estimate how long the food has remained safe. Cooked dal, rice, vegetables, egg, meat, fish, paneer, tofu, curd, milk and cut fruit are all perishable in this sense, however dry or simple they look — a lunchbox needs one of three things: reliable cold, reliable heat, or genuinely shelf-stable food. Worth knowing: The dal and vegetable in today''s lunch are perishable however dry the roti looks — if it''s going into a tiffin, it still needs to be kept properly cold or reheated piping hot, not simply trusted to ''hold up'' because it''s wrapped in roti.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with finely ground roasted peanut powder and a squeeze of lime, if peanut has already been introduced and tolerated", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with finely ground roasted peanut powder and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side, if peanut has already been introduced and tolerated", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed How to pack this: Pack cold — chill all perishable components thoroughly before packing, including the dal, vegetable and chicken, and place them in an insulated bag with two adequately frozen cold sources positioned around the food. The roti can be packed separately at room temperature; a piping-hot preheated container also works if that suits your routine better.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe chikoo (sapota), seeds removed and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Steamed and mashed sweet potato with a pinch of cinnamon, mixed into a small bowl of warm milk", "dinner_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "dinner_b": "Rice with a chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Rice, dal and keeping cooked food properly cold or hot", "notes": "Rice deserves specific care in a lunchbox. Bacillus cereus, a bacterium that survives ordinary cooking as a heat-resistant spore, can multiply in cooked rice left at warm temperatures and produce a toxin that isn''t destroyed by reheating — a documented outbreak affected two day-care centres where rice had been cooled at room temperature and held for hours before serving. Rice isn''t the only concern, though; cooked dal, egg, meat, fish, paneer, tofu and vegetables need the same care. Cool cooked food promptly, in shallow portions, and refrigerate it rather than letting it cool at room temperature. Then either pack it straight from the fridge with two adequately frozen cold sources — two gel packs, or one gel pack plus a frozen water bottle — placed around the food in an insulated bag, or reheat it until piping hot and transfer it immediately into a clean, preheated insulated food container, sealed until lunchtime. To preheat the container, fill it with boiling water, close it and leave it for a few minutes, following the manufacturer''s instructions; empty it immediately before adding the food reheated until piping hot, then close it promptly and keep it sealed until lunch. ''Same-day'' alone doesn''t make food safe — how it''s cooled and packed does. Worth knowing: For today''s rice dish, refrigerate promptly after cooking rather than letting it cool on the counter — pack it straight from the fridge with two frozen cold sources, or reheat it until piping hot and send it in a preheated insulated container.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal cheela with grated carrot and a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal cheela with grated carrot and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked colocasia (arbi), cooked thoroughly and mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked colocasia (arbi), cooked thoroughly and mashed How to pack this: Pack hot — reheat until piping hot and transfer immediately to a preheated insulated food container, sealed until lunchtime; or refrigerate promptly after cooking and pack cold with two frozen sources instead. Either way, don''t leave it cooling on the counter first.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe peach (aadu), peeled, stone removed and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted makhana, finely crushed and softened for the toddler, with a small bowl of warm milk", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked methi (fenugreek leaves), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked methi (fenugreek leaves), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "What can travel without temperature control, and what cannot", "notes": "A food''s texture doesn''t tell you whether it needs temperature control — only what it actually is does. Genuinely shelf-stable options include whole, uncut fruit; plain roti or bread eaten on its own; and packaged foods specifically labelled as not requiring refrigeration. Almost everything else you''d actually pack for a meal — cooked dal, cooked vegetables, rice, egg, meat, fish, paneer, tofu, curd, milk, fresh chutney and cut fruit — is perishable and needs the same cold-or-hot handling as any other cooked food, even a roti-wrapped dal that looks dry and simple. When you''re unsure whether something counts as shelf-stable, it''s safest to treat it as perishable and use temperature control. Worth knowing: The chana dal and broad beans in today''s paratha are perishable, just like a rice dish would be — pack them cold or hot using the same rules as any other cooked food, rather than assuming the paratha wrapper makes it safe at room temperature.", "breakfast_a": "Suji (rava) dhokla, steamed, with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Suji (rava) dhokla, steamed, with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with a chana dal and soft-cooked broad beans (sem), mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with a chana dal and finely crumbled paneer, with soft-cooked broad beans (sem), mashed How to pack this: Pack cold — chill all perishable components thoroughly before packing, including the dal, vegetable and paneer, and place them in an insulated bag with two adequately frozen cold sources positioned around the food. The paratha itself can be packed at room temperature, but what''s inside it can''t be treated the same way.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe pineapple, core removed and finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely grated or crumbled paneer with soft, ripe banana, served separately", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal and soft-cooked dill leaves (suva bhaji), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked dill leaves (suva bhaji), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Choking risks — and why daycare still needs active supervision", "notes": "A tiffin travels without you there, but that calls for more care in what you pack, not less supervision at the table — a toddler still needs to sit upright and be actively watched while eating, whoever is doing the watching, and it''s worth confirming your child''s daycare does this. The standard choking-risk list applies everywhere your toddler eats: quarter whole grapes and cherry tomatoes lengthwise rather than just halving them; avoid whole or chopped nuts, popcorn, hard candy, whole corn kernels, hard raw fruit or vegetables, and sticky spoonfuls of nut butter; cut sausages or similar foods into short strips rather than coins; and finely grate, crumble or shred paneer or cheese rather than packing cubes or large pieces.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated bottle gourd (lauki) and a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Wheat dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated bottle gourd (lauki) and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with an arhar dal and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with an arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped How to pack this: Pack hot in a preheated insulated container, or refrigerate promptly and pack cold with two frozen sources — either works for a mixed grain-and-dal dish like this one.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with seedless grapes, quartered lengthwise into narrow pieces and any seeds removed", "nourishment_break_b": "Mashed banana with finely ground sesame stirred through, if sesame has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft idli with a mild sambar, made with a moong dal and soft-cooked apple gourd (tinda), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft idli with a mild sambar, made with a moong dal and soft-cooked apple gourd (tinda), finely chopped, with finely crumbled paneer stirred through If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "What actually needs to come back empty — and what doesn''t", "notes": "A packed lunch doesn''t need to come back empty, and often won''t — a toddler''s intake commonly varies between meals and from day to day, and lunch is only one part of the day''s overall nourishment. A study of preschoolers'' packed lunches found a meaningful share of what was packed came back uneaten; judge the pattern across several days rather than expecting every tiffin to return empty. What matters more is what happens to the leftover food: don''t offer returned perishable food later unless you know it was kept properly refrigerated at daycare and its temperature history is reliable. When that isn''t clear, it''s safest to discard what''s left rather than serve it again — packing a modest portion in the first place keeps this from meaning much waste.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi cheela with grated zucchini and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi cheela with grated zucchini and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated How to pack this: Pack hot — khichdi travels especially well in a preheated insulated food container; alternatively, refrigerate promptly and pack cold with two frozen sources.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe watermelon, deseeded and cut into small, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Boiled and mashed sweet corn kernels, mixed into a small bowl of warm milk", "dinner_a": "Soft appam with a mild vegetable stew made with soft-cooked potato, carrot and green peas (matar)", "dinner_b": "Soft appam with a mild, tender chicken or mutton stew, fully cooked and free of bones or bone fragments, with soft-cooked potato and carrot If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Skipping the bento-box pressure, and a proper plan for allergies", "notes": "A lunchbox doesn''t need to be elaborately arranged, shaped or decorated to be doing its job well, and there''s no need to feel behind if that isn''t your thing. Feeding guidance is consistent here: your role is deciding what food is offered; your toddler''s role is deciding whether and how much of it to eat, and pressuring, bribing or rewarding them to finish tends to work against learning to eat well, not for it. If your toddler has a diagnosed food allergy, a short note isn''t enough — give the daycare a written emergency care or allergy action plan, any prescribed emergency medicine with clear storage and access instructions, emergency contacts, and confirmation of which staff are trained to recognise a reaction and follow the plan. It''s also worth discussing food-sharing, cross-contact and the daycare''s own allergy policy directly.", "breakfast_a": "Neer dosa with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Neer dosa with soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with a mixed dal and soft-cooked green peas (matar), mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with a mixed dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked green peas (matar), mashed How to pack this: Pack hot in a preheated insulated container, or chill thoroughly and pack cold with two frozen sources — rice specifically should not be left to cool at room temperature before packing.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe dragon fruit (kamalam), mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground cashew mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if cashew has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Packing today''s lunchbox", "notes": "This week''s guidance comes down to one governing rule: perishable food needs to be kept reliably cold, kept reliably hot, or genuinely shelf-stable — dry texture alone doesn''t make cooked food safe at room temperature for hours. In practice: chill food before packing and use two adequately frozen cold sources, or send it piping hot in a preheated insulated container; treat rice with particular care; keep the standard choking precautions in place; and don''t offer back perishable leftovers of uncertain temperature history. None of this needs a special shopping list — just applying the same rule consistently to whatever you''re already packing.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain dosa with grated beetroot and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Multigrain dosa with grated beetroot and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, finely crumbled paneer and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped How to pack this: Pack cold — chill all perishable components thoroughly before packing, including the dal, vegetable and paneer, and place them in an insulated bag with two adequately frozen cold sources positioned around the food. The roti can be packed at room temperature.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe apricot (khubani), stone removed and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pistachio mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if pistachio has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked raw jackfruit (kathal), finely chopped and cooked until soft", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked raw jackfruit (kathal), finely chopped and cooked until soft If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Which one change — packing two cold sources instead of one, trying a hot-food container, or discarding uncertain leftovers instead of re-offering them — is most worth making first?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'A tiffin doesn''t need to be perfect, elaborate, or fully eaten to be doing its job. One rule covers most of the real risk — real cold or real heat, not dry texture — and the rest is reasonable expectations, not more effort.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 119: How much magnesium does your toddler need, and where does it come from?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   119,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much magnesium does your toddler need, and where does it come from?',
   '"Magnesium supports your toddler''s bones, muscles, nerves and everyday energy — and regularly offering legumes, grains, vegetables, dairy or suitable alternatives, and safely prepared nuts or seeds can support their intake without turning each meal into a calculation."',
   'Magnesium supports your toddler''s bone development, muscle and nerve function, and everyday energy metabolism — a genuinely essential mineral, even though it gets far less attention than calcium or iron. It''s present in dal, legumes, whole grains, milk, leafy greens and a few nuts or seeds, which offer several regular sources rather than depending on one special food. Isolated deficiency is uncommon outside a specific medical reason, a healthy toddler doesn''t need a supplement, and some of magnesium''s popular claims — particularly around children''s sleep — are more uncertain than the marketing suggests. This week walks through what''s proven, what''s still unsettled, and one household safety point worth knowing about magnesium-based products. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. Most medical-condition notes below apply to the mother. The first note is specifically about the toddler and signals when individual paediatric guidance is needed.',
   'If your toddler has coeliac disease, chronic diarrhoea, a very restricted intake, or another diagnosed gastrointestinal condition: this may affect how well magnesium and other nutrients are absorbed, and can justify individual assessment — it doesn''t mean every child with one of these conditions has a magnesium deficiency, but it''s worth discussing directly with your paediatrician rather than trying to address it through food choices alone. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn magnesium into something to track. A few everyday habits — dal, whole grains, milk, greens and a spoon of ground nuts or seeds — can help meet magnesium needs. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————- Meat, fish, eggs and dairy have no special relevance to whether a meal is nutritious this week — the dal, whole grains, leafy greens, milk and nuts or seeds in this week''s menu provide magnesium regardless of which protein option you choose. Wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————-',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "What magnesium actually does for a growing toddler", "notes": "Magnesium is a mineral your toddler''s body uses constantly, though it rarely gets the attention that iron or calcium do. It''s a cofactor for hundreds of the enzyme systems that keep the body running: building the mineral structure of bone alongside calcium and phosphorus, allowing muscles to contract and relax normally, supporting normal nerve signalling, and helping convert food into usable energy. None of this requires you to track a daily number — it''s simply useful to know what this mineral is quietly doing in the background of ordinary growth.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) porridge with mashed banana and a pinch of cardamom", "breakfast_b": "Ragi (finger millet) porridge with mashed banana and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe papaya, deseeded and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground almond mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if almond has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a rajma (kidney beans), well mashed, and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu)", "dinner_b": "Rice with a rajma (kidney beans), well mashed, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu) If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "How much magnesium your toddler needs — and why the number matters less than variety", "notes": "One commonly used reference value is 80 mg of magnesium a day for children aged one to three — the United States'' Recommended Dietary Allowance. Reference systems can differ, and this number is not a target parents need to calculate meal by meal. A varied eating pattern that regularly includes legumes, whole grains, vegetables, dairy or suitable alternatives, and safely prepared nuts or seeds can contribute magnesium across the day. Portions and appetites vary, so the aim is repeated variety rather than guaranteeing that any particular menu automatically meets an exact number.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable oats khichdi with grated carrot and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable oats khichdi with grated carrot and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with a chana dal and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with a chana dal and a fully cooked egg curry, with soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe guava, deseeded and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground sesame (til) mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if sesame has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with an arhar dal and soft-cooked moringa (drumstick) leaves, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with an arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked moringa (drumstick) leaves, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where magnesium already lives in an everyday Indian diet", "notes": "Magnesium shows up in foods that likely already anchor your toddler''s everyday plate. Dal and legumes — moong, chana, masoor, arhar and rajma — are a steady source. Whole grains, including whole wheat, bajra, jowar, ragi, brown rice and oats, contribute more than their refined counterparts. Milk and curd add a smaller but real amount. Leafy greens such as spinach, moringa (drumstick) leaves and amaranth leaves, and a spoonful of finely ground nuts or seeds — peanut, almond, sesame or pumpkin seed — in a nourishment break round it out. Jaggery, despite a common belief, contains only a trace amount and isn''t a meaningful source. None of this requires a special shopping list — a typical home-cooked Indian toddler diet usually offers several of these sources already, which usually makes precise meal-by-meal counting unnecessary.", "breakfast_a": "Jowar (sorghum) dosa, soft, with a smooth, mild peanut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Jowar (sorghum) dosa, soft, with finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Brown rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki)", "lunch_b": "Brown rice with a masoor dal, finely crumbled paneer, and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki)", "nourishment_break_a": "Mashed banana with finely ground flaxseed stirred through", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pumpkin seed mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if pumpkin seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mixed dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Is a magnesium deficiency something to worry about?", "notes": "Isolated dietary magnesium deficiency is uncommon in an otherwise healthy toddler eating a varied diet. Risk is more relevant when a child has prolonged gastrointestinal losses, impaired absorption, certain medical conditions, particular medicines or a very restricted diet. These situations need individual medical assessment rather than self-treatment with supplements.", "breakfast_a": "Bajra (pearl millet) upma with grated vegetables and a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Bajra (pearl millet) upma with grated vegetables and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe sitaphal (custard apple), deseeded and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground roasted chana mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if chana has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft idli with a mild sambar made with arhar or moong dal and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft idli with a mild sambar made with arhar or moong dal and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped, with a fully cooked egg, finely chopped, on the side If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Magnesium supplements, and a household safety point worth knowing", "notes": "A healthy toddler eating a varied diet does not need a magnesium supplement, and routine supplementation isn''t recommended outside specific medical situations your paediatrician would identify. For children aged one to three, the US upper limit is 65 mg a day from supplements and medications. It does not include magnesium naturally present in food. Supplements or magnesium-containing medicines should not be given above a clinician-directed dose. Magnesium naturally present in ordinary foods is not expected to cause harm in a healthy child. The more practical safety point is a household one: magnesium-based products such as flavoured magnesium citrate laxatives and fragranced Epsom salts can look and smell appealing to a curious toddler, and swallowing a meaningful amount can cause real harm. Worth knowing: Keep magnesium-containing supplements, laxatives, antacids and fragranced Epsom salts locked or stored up, away and out of reach, just like medicines. If your toddler may have swallowed one, do not wait for symptoms and do not induce vomiting. Contact your local poison-information service or emergency medical service promptly, keeping the product container available so the exact ingredient and amount can be identified.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat dalia (broken wheat) porridge with finely ground roasted peanut powder, if peanut has already been introduced and tolerated", "breakfast_b": "Wheat dalia (broken wheat) porridge with soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe muskmelon (kharbuja), deseeded and diced", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground chironji (charoli) mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if chironji has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a rajma (kidney beans), well mashed, and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a rajma (kidney beans), well mashed, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Magnesium, sleep and constipation — what''s proven and what''s marketing", "notes": "Magnesium is sometimes marketed as a fix for children''s sleep, mood or constipation, and it''s worth separating what''s established from what''s promoted. Magnesium supplements are widely promoted for sleep, but evidence for improving sleep is limited and inconsistent, and direct evidence in toddlers is insufficient. Most available trials and reviews concern adults, so they cannot be used to promise either benefit or a precise sleep effect in young children. Persistent sleep difficulty deserves a discussion about the child''s routine, health and development rather than an unprescribed magnesium supplement. For constipation: as a medication, magnesium — in products like magnesium citrate or Milk of Magnesia — is a genuine, well-established laxative, but that''s a different thing from the everyday magnesium in your toddler''s dal and roti, and there''s no good evidence that ordinary dietary magnesium meaningfully affects everyday constipation at food-level amounts; a magnesium laxative should be used for a toddler only when a qualified clinician has advised the specific product and dose. None of this makes magnesium unimportant — it just means a good night''s sleep or regular bowel habits are unlikely to come from a supplement bottle rather than the basics: a consistent routine, and a varied diet.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain dosa with grated beetroot and a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Multigrain dosa with grated beetroot and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe pear, peeled, cored and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground til (sesame) mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if sesame has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a mixed dal and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mixed dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Bringing it together: dal, a whole grain, and something from the greens-or-nuts list", "notes": "This week''s takeaway is simple: dal, whole grains, milk and a spoon of ground nuts or seeds can help meet a toddler''s magnesium needs across a varied diet, so there''s usually no need to calculate an exact number or reach for a supplement. A magnesium deficiency worth worrying about is uncommon in an otherwise healthy toddler and is more relevant with a specific medical reason, not everyday eating — and any magnesium-based laxative, supplement or Epsom salt product at home belongs locked or stored up, away and out of reach, the same way as any other medicine.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) cheela with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi (finger millet) cheela with finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, finely crumbled paneer, and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft orange segments with seeds and tough membranes removed, cut into small manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground flaxseed mixed into a small bowl of warm milk", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Which one thing is most worth doing — keeping a laxative or Epsom salt product safely out of reach, adding a spoon of ground nuts or seeds to a nourishment break, or simply letting go of tracking an exact number?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Magnesium is another nutrient that usually comes from an overall eating pattern rather than one special food. Regularly offering legumes, grains, vegetables, dairy or suitable alternatives, and safely prepared nuts or seeds can support a toddler''s intake without turning each meal into a calculation or reaching automatically for a supplement.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
