-- postpartum week 120: Cow's milk allergy or lactose intolerance? Telling them apart, and what to serve your toddler instead
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   120,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Cow''s milk allergy or lactose intolerance? Telling them apart, and what to serve your toddler instead',
   '"Milk allergy and lactose intolerance are different problems with different causes — telling them apart matters, because the right response, and what safely replaces milk, depends on which one it actually is."',
   '"My toddler can''t have milk" can mean two genuinely different things. Milk allergy is the immune system reacting to milk protein, sometimes rapidly and sometimes over hours or days. Lactose intolerance is a digestive problem — not enough of the enzyme that breaks down milk sugar — causing bloating, gas or loose stools, with no allergic or breathing symptoms involved. Primary lactose intolerance is uncommon at toddler age, though exactly when it becomes noticeable varies by genetics and population; what looks like it in a toddler is often a temporary dip after a stomach bug, or another digestive cause worth an actual assessment. Getting an actual diagnosis — not a guess, and not an unproven test — matters, because an undiagnosed dairy restriction can affect a toddler''s growth, calcium and vitamin D, and because the right substitute genuinely differs depending on which condition it is. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely. The medical-condition notes below open with two notes about the toddler, specific to this week''s topic; the rest apply to the mother.',
   'If your toddler is having a severe reaction right now: breathing difficulty, wheezing, marked facial or tongue swelling, collapse or unusual limpness — use prescribed adrenaline/epinephrine if available and call emergency medical services immediately; don''t wait for a routine appointment. If your toddler has previously had hives, lip or facial swelling, wheezing or breathing difficulty after milk — or repetitive vomiting, especially when accompanied by another allergic symptom — and the reaction has resolved: treat this as a possible allergy and see your paediatrician before offering dairy again — this needs an actual evaluation, not a wait-and-see approach. If your toddler has a diagnosed milk allergy: follow your allergist''s specific avoidance and replacement plan exactly; ghee, curd and paneer are not automatically safe just because they''re gentler on lactose digestion, since the concern with an allergy is the protein, not the sugar. If your toddler has diagnosed or suspected lactose intolerance: this is often temporary, especially if it followed a stomach bug, but persistent symptoms deserve an actual assessment rather than an assumption they''ll resolve on their own — reintroducing dairy gradually as your paediatrician advises, rather than removing it indefinitely, is usually the right next step once that assessment is done. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn every meal into detective work. A clear diagnosis, from the right professional, is what actually tells you which foods are the concern. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. How to use this menu if dairy is a concern —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— For diagnosed lactose intolerance — or while following a clinician-advised trial — labelled lactose-free milk or lactose-free curd may replace the corresponding dairy items if tolerated. Some children also tolerate ordinary paneer, but its lactose content varies and individual tolerance should guide its use. For diagnosed milk-protein allergy: replace milk with the approved fortified dairy-free alternative, and replace paneer or curd with a protein-rich food — egg, dal, a lean meat/fish option or tofu, if tolerated — not a plant drink alone. If nothing is diagnosed yet: do not begin a prolonged dairy restriction for unassessed symptoms; get an assessment first, and use each day''s "Condition swap" note only once you know which condition, if any, applies. Wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Two different problems that get lumped together as \"can''t have milk\"", "notes": "Milk allergy and lactose intolerance get lumped together as \"can''t have milk,\" but almost nothing else about them is similar. Milk allergy is the immune system reacting to milk protein — mainly casein and whey — as if it were a threat; this can happen quickly (an IgE-mediated reaction) or develop over hours to days (a non-IgE-mediated reaction), and either form is a genuine immune response, not a digestive quirk. Lactose intolerance has nothing to do with the immune system: it''s a shortage of lactase, the enzyme that breaks down milk sugar, so undigested lactose ferments in the gut and causes discomfort. Both can cause an upset stomach after drinking milk, which is exactly why they''re confused — but which one your toddler actually has changes what happens next. Condition swap: Use labelled lactose-free milk or curd for diagnosed lactose intolerance if tolerated; ordinary paneer may be tolerated by some children, but this varies. For diagnosed milk allergy, use the child''s fortified dairy-free beverage instead of milk, and swap curd or paneer for a protein-rich food, not a plant drink alone.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable semiya (vermicelli) upma with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable semiya (vermicelli) upma with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan), mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked brinjal (baingan), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe mango, stone removed and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground walnut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if walnut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked turnip (shalgam), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, dairy that is safe for your child''s diagnosed condition, the child''s approved fortified alternative, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Reading the timeline: what''s fast, what''s slow, and what needs urgent attention", "notes": "The timing and pattern of symptoms is the clearest clue. An immediate (IgE-mediated) milk allergy reaction appears within minutes to about two hours: hives, swelling of the lips or face, vomiting, wheeze, and in a severe reaction, a whole-body response (anaphylaxis) that is a medical emergency. Delayed (non-IgE-mediated) milk-allergy symptoms can include persistent gastrointestinal symptoms, blood in the stool, eczema associated with other symptoms, feeding difficulty or faltering growth over hours to days — but these findings are not specific to milk allergy; they need clinical assessment, because many common childhood conditions can look similar. Lactose intolerance causes bloating, gas and watery stools roughly 30 minutes to two hours after dairy, without hives, swelling or breathing difficulty. Worth knowing: If your toddler is having breathing difficulty, wheezing, marked facial or tongue swelling, collapse, unusual limpness or another severe reaction right now, use prescribed adrenaline/epinephrine if available and call local emergency medical services immediately — don''t wait for a routine appointment. If a reaction like this happened previously and has resolved, avoid offering the suspected food again until your toddler has been medically evaluated. Condition swap: Use labelled lactose-free milk or curd for diagnosed lactose intolerance if tolerated; ordinary paneer may be tolerated by some children, but this varies. For diagnosed milk allergy, use the child''s fortified dairy-free beverage instead of milk, and swap curd or paneer for a protein-rich food, not a plant drink alone.", "breakfast_a": "Besan (gram flour) chilla with grated zucchini and a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Besan (gram flour) chilla with grated zucchini and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked ivy gourd (tindora), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked ivy gourd (tindora), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe kiwi, skin removed and finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground roasted peanut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if peanut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked cluster beans (gawar), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, dairy that is safe for your child''s diagnosed condition, the child''s approved fortified alternative, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Why \"lactose intolerant\" is said more often than it''s actually true at this age", "notes": "Here''s a genuinely useful correction: true, ongoing (primary) lactose intolerance is uncommon in very young children, but the age at which it becomes noticeable varies with genetics and population — symptoms may begin in later childhood, adolescence or adulthood, rather than following one universal timeline, and it isn''t actually a common toddler diagnosis, even though the phrase gets used loosely. What often gets called \"lactose intolerance\" in a toddler this age is more commonly something else: a temporary, secondary lactose intolerance following a stomach bug or another condition that injures the small-intestinal lining, which usually eases as the gut heals, or a different digestive cause altogether — sometimes including a milk allergy with mostly digestive symptoms, among other possibilities. Persistent symptoms need an actual assessment rather than an assumption that they''ll resolve on their own. Condition swap: Use labelled lactose-free milk or curd for diagnosed lactose intolerance if tolerated; ordinary paneer may be tolerated by some children, but this varies. For diagnosed milk allergy, use the child''s fortified dairy-free beverage instead of milk, and swap curd or paneer for a protein-rich food, not a plant drink alone.", "breakfast_a": "Soft idli with a smooth, mild tomato-coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Soft idli with finely crumbled paneer mixed into a smooth, mild tomato-coconut chutney", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with a chana dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with a chana dal and a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, with soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe litchi, skin and seed removed, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground roasted moong dal mixed into a small bowl of warm milk", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal and soft-cooked raw banana (kacha kela), finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked raw banana (kacha kela), finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, dairy that is safe for your child''s diagnosed condition, the child''s approved fortified alternative, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Getting an actual diagnosis — and the home test worth skipping", "notes": "An actual diagnosis matters more than it might seem, and the pathway depends on what''s suspected. For a suspected immediate allergic reaction: history and examination come first, and skin-prick or specific-IgE blood testing may support the diagnosis, though neither test proves allergy on its own — a supervised oral food challenge may be used when clinically appropriate, and should never be attempted at home. For a suspected delayed allergic reaction: diagnosis may involve a clinician-directed elimination followed by a planned reintroduction, with the approach depending on the reaction history, and a severe previous reaction should never be challenged at home. For suspected lactose intolerance: assessment may include a symptom history, a clinician-guided lactose reduction and reintroduction, or in some cases a hydrogen breath test. One test worth actively avoiding regardless of what''s suspected: IgG or IgG4 \"food sensitivity\" panels, which major allergy societies explicitly state are not validated for diagnosing food allergy or intolerance and shouldn''t determine your toddler''s exclusions. Condition swap: Use labelled lactose-free milk or curd for diagnosed lactose intolerance if tolerated; ordinary paneer may be tolerated by some children, but this varies. For diagnosed milk allergy, use the child''s fortified dairy-free beverage instead of milk, and swap curd or paneer for a protein-rich food, not a plant drink alone.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam with grated carrot and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable uttapam with grated carrot and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with an arhar dal and soft-cooked yellow pumpkin, mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with an arhar dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked yellow pumpkin, mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe plum, stone removed and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely grated coconut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk", "dinner_a": "Soft idli with a mild sambar made with arhar dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft idli with a mild sambar made with arhar dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely chopped, with a fully cooked egg, finely chopped, on the side If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, dairy that is safe for your child''s diagnosed condition, the child''s approved fortified alternative, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "If milk is confirmed unsafe: what actually replaces it", "notes": "What safely replaces milk depends on which condition it actually is. For lactose intolerance, lactose-free cow''s milk and lactose-free dairy products can work well if tolerated — the lactose is removed or already broken down, while the protein, calcium and other nutrients of ordinary dairy stay largely intact. For milk-protein allergy, lactose-free milk is not safe, since it still contains the protein that''s actually the problem — and goat, sheep or buffalo milk aren''t automatically safe alternatives either, since their proteins can cross-react with cow''s milk protein. Plant-based beverages vary enormously in protein, fat, energy, calcium and vitamin D, so fortification with calcium and vitamin D alone doesn''t make a drink equivalent — check protein and energy on the label too, not just the two headline nutrients. Fortified soy beverage is generally the closest common nutritional match to cow''s milk, but soy still has to be individually confirmed appropriate for your toddler, not assumed safe. Homemade nut, seed, oat or rice drinks are not nutritionally equivalent replacements. If your toddler has more than one restriction, is growing slowly, or eats a limited range of foods, this is worth an individual replacement plan with your paediatrician or a dietitian rather than a general list. Worth knowing: Some children later turn out to tolerate milk in a well-baked form, such as a biscuit or muffin, even while reacting to fresh milk. A baked-milk introduction or \"milk ladder\" should begin only when your child''s allergy clinician has confirmed it''s appropriate and has specified the starting food, portion and progression — it''s not a home test to try independently, even with an ordinary biscuit. Condition swap: For confirmed lactose intolerance, lactose-free milk or curd may be used if tolerated. For milk-protein allergy, lactose-free dairy is not suitable.", "breakfast_a": "Rava (semolina) upma with mixed vegetables and a squeeze of lime", "breakfast_b": "Rava (semolina) upma with mixed vegetables and soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and soft-cooked ash gourd (petha), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked ash gourd (petha), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "Soft, ripe strawberry, hulled and finely chopped, with the child''s approved unsweetened fortified dairy alternative, served separately", "nourishment_break_b": "Steamed and mashed apple with finely ground sesame or almond stirred through, if already introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft appam with a mild vegetable stew made with soft-cooked potato, carrot and drumstick (moringa), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft appam with a mild, tender chicken or mutton stew, fully cooked and free of bones or bone fragments, with soft-cooked potato and drumstick (moringa), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, dairy that is safe for your child''s diagnosed condition, the child''s approved fortified alternative, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Ghee, curd and paneer — and an important difference between allergy and intolerance here", "notes": "Ghee, curd and paneer bring in an important distinction between the two conditions, though individual tolerance for lactose intolerance still varies from child to child. Ghee contains very little lactose, since the clarification process removes most of the milk solids that carry it — but it may retain trace milk protein and isn''t automatically safe for milk allergy. Curd may be better tolerated by some people with lactose intolerance than plain milk, but it still contains lactose, and how much is tolerated varies. Paneer generally contains less lactose than milk, but the exact amount varies and tolerance is individual, not guaranteed. All three remain milk-derived foods, and for milk-protein allergy, none of them are safe unless your toddler''s allergy clinician has specifically approved one — treat them exactly like fresh milk unless told otherwise. This is exactly why the diagnosis in Day 4 matters: the two conditions call for genuinely different food choices, not the same dairy-free list. Condition swap: For milk-protein allergy, skip the curd and paneer above: serve the paratha plain, and offer the mosambi alone or the hazelnut in the child''s fortified alternative instead of milk. For lactose intolerance, labelled lactose-free curd may be used if tolerated; ordinary paneer may be tolerated by some children, but this varies.", "breakfast_a": "Aloo (potato) paratha, soft, with a small bowl of plain curd on the side", "breakfast_b": "Aloo (potato) paratha, soft, with finely crumbled paneer mixed into the filling", "lunch_a": "Rice with a mixed dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with a mixed dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe mosambi (sweet lime) segments, seeds and membrane removed, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground hazelnut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if hazelnut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, dairy that is safe for your child''s diagnosed condition, the child''s approved fortified alternative, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "What to actually do if you suspect milk doesn''t agree with your toddler", "notes": "This week''s guidance comes down to one governing move: get an actual diagnosis before changing anything long-term. Write down what was eaten, how soon symptoms appeared, and exactly what they looked like, and bring that to your paediatrician rather than starting an indefinite dairy-free diet on a guess. If your toddler is having a severe reaction right now — breathing difficulty, wheezing, marked facial or tongue swelling, collapse or unusual limpness — use prescribed adrenaline/epinephrine if available and call emergency medical services immediately; don''t wait for a routine appointment. If your toddler has previously had hives, lip or facial swelling, wheezing or breathing difficulty after milk — or repetitive vomiting, especially when accompanied by another allergic symptom — and the reaction has resolved, treat it as a possible allergy and get it evaluated before offering dairy again. Ask about a referral to an allergist or dietitian if things are unclear or your toddler is managing more than one restriction. Once you know which condition it actually is, the right substitute — and the right foods to keep or avoid — follows naturally from there. Condition swap: Use labelled lactose-free milk or curd for diagnosed lactose intolerance if tolerated; ordinary paneer may be tolerated by some children, but this varies. For diagnosed milk allergy, use the child''s fortified dairy-free beverage instead of milk, and swap curd or paneer for a protein-rich food, not a plant drink alone.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with grated apple and a pinch of cinnamon", "breakfast_b": "Oats porridge with grated apple and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked field beans (val), mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, finely crumbled paneer and soft-cooked field beans (val), mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe fig (anjeer), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground chia seed mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if chia has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked pointed gourd (parwal), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked pointed gourd (parwal), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, dairy that is safe for your child''s diagnosed condition, the child''s approved fortified alternative, or another suitable family food. If you''ve wondered whether milk might be behind a symptom you''ve noticed, what''s the one thing worth writing down before your next paediatrician visit — what was eaten, how soon symptoms appeared, and exactly what they looked like?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   '"Can''t have milk" isn''t one thing, and treating it as one thing is exactly what leaves families stuck — either over-restricting foods that were never the problem, or under-protecting against one that genuinely is. Milk allergy and lactose intolerance need different answers: different foods, different urgency, and sometimes different specialists. The only way to know which one you''re dealing with is an actual diagnosis, not a guess and not an elimination diet started on your own. Once you know, the right substitute — and the foods that were never actually the problem — both become clear.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 121: How much folate does your toddler need, and where does it come from?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   121,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much folate does your toddler need, and where does it come from?',
   '"Folate supports the everyday work of growing — new cells, new blood and new tissue — and familiar foods such as dal can make a meaningful contribution."',
   'Folate, also called vitamin B9, is what your body uses to build DNA and to let cells divide — work that never stops during toddlerhood''s rapid growth and constant blood-cell renewal. The amount a toddler actually needs is modest: roughly 120-150 micrograms a day, depending on which reference you use, a target that varied meals containing pulses, vegetables and fruit can help meet without special foods or routine tracking. Dal, one of the most everyday foods in an Indian kitchen, is one of the single best sources of folate available — a toddler-sized portion contributes meaningfully. Leafy greens and a few fruits add more, though how you cook them changes how much folate survives. True folate deficiency can occur, but available findings from selected clinical populations should not be treated as prevalence estimates for all Indian toddlers, and this week also covers the one supplement-safety nuance worth understanding accurately, rather than either ignoring it or over-worrying about it. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely.',
   'If your toddler has coeliac disease, chronic diarrhoea, a malabsorption condition, or is on medication that can affect folate levels: this may affect how well folate and other nutrients are absorbed or used, and can justify individual assessment — it doesn''t mean every child in this situation has a folate deficiency, but it''s worth discussing directly with your paediatrician rather than trying to address it through food choices alone. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn every meal into detective work. A clear diagnosis, from the right professional, is what actually tells you which foods are the concern. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— This week''s menu leans on dal, legumes, leafy greens and fruit — not because anything unusual is needed, but to make the point visible: an ordinary Indian family meal is already doing real folate work. Wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully. ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "What folate actually does, and how much your toddler needs", "notes": "Folate''s job is unglamorous but constant: it''s what your body needs to build DNA and let cells divide, which matters everywhere growth is happening — new blood cells, new gut lining, new everything, all the time, at this age. The actual amount a toddler needs is small. The US reference (NIH) sets it at 150 micrograms a day; India''s ICMR-NIN 2020 guidelines set it at 120 micrograms a day for this age band. The two numbers are close, though they may not be measured in exactly the same way, so treat them as roughly aligned rather than precisely comparable. Either way, it is a modest target, and a varied pattern containing pulses, vegetables and fruit can contribute substantially without requiring special foods or daily nutrient calculations.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal and rice dosa (pesarattu), soft, with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal and rice dosa (pesarattu), soft, with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe chikoo (sapota), peeled and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground cashew mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if cashew has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a mixed dal and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mixed dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Dal and legumes — your toddler''s best everyday folate source", "notes": "If you wanted to choose one familiar Indian food group that quietly contributes substantial folate, pulses would be an excellent place to begin. Lentils, chickpeas, rajma and lobia all provide folate, although the amount varies by pulse, variety, preparation, serving size and the food-composition database used. A toddler eats only a fraction of an adult cup, so avoid treating an adult serving value as the child''s intake. The useful everyday message is simpler: an age-appropriate portion of dal or another pulse contributes meaningfully toward folate needs while also providing protein, fibre and other nutrients.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) dosa, soft, with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi (finger millet) dosa, soft, with a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "Rajma (kidney beans), well mashed, mixed into a small bowl of plain curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe watermelon, deseeded and diced", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a lobia (black-eyed peas), well mashed, and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a lobia (black-eyed peas), well mashed, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Leafy greens and fruit — good sources, if you cook them right", "notes": "Leafy greens and fruit add more folate on top of dal, though not all of them equally, and how you cook them matters. Among greens, palak (spinach) is a strong source, with methi, mint and coriander contributing smaller but real amounts. Among fruits, papaya and mango are moderate sources; orange is a bit lower; and banana — often treated as an all-purpose toddler superfood — is actually a fairly modest folate source compared to a spoonful of dal or a katori of cooked greens. Cooking method genuinely changes how much folate survives: it''s water-soluble and breaks down with heat, and a controlled study found that boiling caused real, duration-dependent folate loss in spinach, while steaming did not cause the same loss. Practically, that means steaming or a quick sauté preserves more folate in greens than a long boil, and if you are boiling dal or vegetables, using the cooking water back in the dish — rather than draining and discarding it — keeps some of what leached out.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with finely chopped spinach (palak) and a squeeze of lime", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with finely chopped spinach (palak) and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and lightly steamed methi (fenugreek leaves), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and lightly steamed methi (fenugreek leaves), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe mango, stone removed and finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe papaya, deseeded and finely diced, with a pinch of roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "Soft idli with a mild sambar made with arhar dal and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft idli with a mild sambar made with arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "What folate deficiency actually looks like — and how common it really is", "notes": "True folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anaemia — a specific kind of anaemia with unusually large red blood cells — which can show up as fatigue, weakness, irritability and poor appetite, and sometimes mouth or tongue sores. In growing children, ongoing deficiency can also affect growth. How common is this in an Indian toddler? One hospital-based study of children aged 6-59 months in Haryana found folate deficiency in about 11% of children studied, compared with vitamin B12 deficiency in about 22% — a limited finding from that particular clinical population, not evidence of prevalence across Indian toddlers. A varied diet containing pulses, vegetables and fruit lowers the likelihood of inadequate folate intake, but symptoms or growth concerns still require assessment rather than assumptions based on the menu — and that''s not a reason to assume every other nutrient is automatically covered too; iron and B12, in particular, deserve their own ongoing attention, as covered in earlier weeks of this series.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with roasted peanuts and a squeeze of lime", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with roasted peanuts and soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with an arhar dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "Finely ground roasted moong dal mixed into a small bowl of plain curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe orange segments, seeds and tough membranes removed, finely chopped", "dinner_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked okra (bhindi), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a masoor dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked okra (bhindi), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Fortified atta and rice — check the label rather than assuming", "notes": "India''s Food Safety and Standards (Fortification of Foods) Regulations, 2018 set composition, quality and labelling requirements for atta and rice when manufacturers or public programmes fortify them with iron, folic acid and vitamin B12. They did not make every packet of retail atta or rice universally fortified. Some branded products and government-supplied staples are fortified, while others are not, so check for the +F logo and the nutrition or ingredient label rather than assuming. Fortified staples can make a useful contribution when your household receives or chooses them, but pulses, vegetables and fruit remain important parts of a varied diet.", "breakfast_a": "Soft wheat paratha (from fortified atta, if your household uses it) with a small bowl of plain curd on the side", "breakfast_b": "Soft wheat paratha (from fortified atta, if your household uses it) with finely crumbled paneer mixed into the filling", "lunch_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with a moong dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with a moong dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe guava, deseeded and finely mashed with a pinch of roasted cumin", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pistachio mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if pistachio has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The one supplement caution worth understanding, and India''s IFA programme", "notes": "Large amounts of folic acid from supplements can correct the anaemia caused by vitamin B12 deficiency without treating the associated neurological injury, potentially delaying recognition. This concern relates to supplemental and fortified folic acid, not naturally occurring folate in dal, vegetables or fruit. For children aged 1-3 years, the US tolerable upper intake level is 300 micrograms a day of folic acid from supplements and fortified foods; it does not apply to naturally occurring food folate, and a clinician may prescribe a different amount for a medical reason. Do not combine multivitamins, folic-acid products or iron-folic acid preparations without checking the total with the child''s healthcare team. Under India''s Anaemia Mukt Bharat programme, children aged 6-59 months are included in the prophylactic schedule for 1 ml of iron-folic acid syrup twice weekly, containing 20 mg elemental iron and 100 micrograms folic acid per dose. Access and administration should be confirmed through the local ASHA, anganwadi or healthcare team. Programme guidance includes precautions during acute illness and for children with conditions such as thalassaemia major or a history of repeated blood transfusions, so this should not be started or duplicated independently.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain uttapam with grated carrot and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Multigrain uttapam with grated carrot and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe pear, peeled, cored and finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground walnut mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if walnut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a mixed dal and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a mixed dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Bringing it together — a folate-friendly week without overthinking it", "notes": "This week''s guidance comes down to one simple, reassuring picture: if today''s meals included pulses, vegetables or fruit, they contributed useful folate toward your toddler''s overall needs — without any tracking, special ingredient hunting or supplement. Cooking method matters more than exotic sourcing: steaming or a quick sauté keeps more folate in greens than a long boil, and reusing dal cooking water rather than discarding it helps too. Folic acid fortification of atta and rice, where your household happens to use it, is a welcome bonus, not something to rely on. And if a doctor ever prescribes a high-dose folic acid supplement for your child, it''s reasonable to ask whether vitamin B12 has also been checked — but that''s a clinical conversation, not a reason to worry about the dal on today''s plate.", "breakfast_a": "Soft idli with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Soft idli with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe mosambi (sweet lime) segments, seeds and membrane removed, finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground flaxseed mixed into a small bowl of plain curd", "dinner_a": "Rice with an arhar dal and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with an arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Thinking back over this week''s meals, how many days already included dal, a leafy green or a folate-rich fruit — and did that feel like effort, or like an ordinary Tuesday?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Folate isn''t a nutrient that asks for anything extra — it asks you to notice what''s already there. Dal, greens and fruit can contribute useful folate through ordinary family meals, without turning eating into a daily calculation.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 122: How safe is your toddler's drinking water? RO purifiers, TDS, and what "safe" really means
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   122,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How safe is your toddler''s drinking water? RO purifiers, TDS, and what "safe" really means',
   '"Water safety is about what is actually in the water — a purifier''s TDS number, on its own, does not tell you that."',
   'Most households give real thought to what a toddler eats, but the water behind every meal — used for cooking, mixing, and drinking on its own — often gets less scrutiny than the number glowing on a purifier''s display. That number, TDS (total dissolved solids), is widely treated as a safety score, but it isn''t one: the World Health Organization sets no health-based guideline for it at all, treating it only as a taste indicator. The meaningful safety questions include whether harmful microbes are present and whether source-specific chemicals — such as fluoride, nitrate, arsenic or lead — exceed applicable drinking-water limits. Which parameters matter depends on the source, local information and household plumbing. This week separates what''s actually worth knowing about your household''s water from what a purifier''s display number does and doesn''t tell you, without turning it into another source of worry. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely.',
   'If your toddler has a kidney condition, a heart condition, or another condition affecting fluid or mineral intake: their appropriate water source, volume and mineral intake may need individual guidance from their treating specialist — this week''s general information isn''t a substitute for that. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn every glass of water into detective work. A clear answer about your own water source, from an actual test, is what actually tells you which of this week''s concerns apply to your household. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— This week''s focus is on the water in your kitchen rather than any single food. Use your household''s usual safe drinking-water source for cooking, mixing and rinsing produce; wash all produce thoroughly with that same water, and cook ingredients fully. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Three separate questions, not one: microbial, chemical, and TDS", "notes": "Drinking water safety is best understood as three separate questions, not one. The first and most urgent is microbial: are there bacteria, viruses or parasites present that could cause acute illness? The second is chemical: are specific substances such as fluoride, arsenic, nitrate or pesticide residue present at levels that matter — this depends heavily on your particular region and water source, not on India as a whole. The third is dissolved solids, usually summarised as TDS, a number many purifiers display prominently, which mostly reflects taste and mineral content rather than safety. A common assumption is that a lower TDS number means safer water. It doesn''t necessarily — TDS on its own says nothing about whether bacteria or a specific toxic chemical is present. This week walks through what actually matters for each of these three questions, and what''s realistic to check for your own household.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with roasted peanuts and a squeeze of lime", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with roasted peanuts and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe banana, mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground almond mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if almond has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Rice with a masoor dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "TDS: what a purifier''s number does and doesn''t tell you", "notes": "India''s Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS IS 10500:2012) sets an acceptable limit for TDS in drinking water of 500 mg/l, with a permissible limit of 2,000 mg/l in the absence of an alternative source. The World Health Organization goes further and sets no health-based guideline value for TDS at all — it treats TDS purely as an acceptability parameter, meaning high levels can affect taste and be objectionable to consumers, not that a specific level is a proven health hazard. In practice, this means a purifier''s TDS display is a taste and mineral-content indicator, not a safety certificate: water with a very low TDS reading can still carry bacteria or a specific chemical contaminant if microbial or chemical treatment wasn''t effective, and water with a moderate TDS reading may or may not be safe, depending on its microbial quality and individual chemical constituents. If you want a genuine safety read on your water, TDS alone will not give you one.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) porridge with grated apple and a pinch of cinnamon", "breakfast_b": "Ragi (finger millet) porridge with a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with a chana dal and soft-cooked okra (bhindi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with a chana dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked okra (bhindi), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "Finely ground roasted chana (bhuna chana) mixed into a small bowl of plain curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe custard apple (sitaphal), deseeded and mashed", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "How RO, UV and UF perform different jobs", "notes": "Reverse osmosis forces water through a semipermeable membrane. According to the US CDC, an appropriately designed and functioning RO system can remove parasites, bacteria and viruses and can reduce certain dissolved chemicals, but the exact chemicals removed depend on the particular system and its verified performance claims. UV treatment disinfects water but does not remove dissolved chemicals. Ultrafiltration removes parasites and bacteria, is only somewhat effective against viruses, and does not remove dissolved chemicals. Some home purifiers combine technologies because each performs a different job, but more stages do not automatically make a unit suitable for every water source. Product design — including whether any blending or bypass mechanism is used — varies by model. Families should check the manufacturer''s flow diagram, verified contaminant-reduction claims and maintenance instructions, and should not assume that a TDS adjustment feature makes untreated source water safe.", "breakfast_a": "Soft idli with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Soft idli with finely crumbled paneer mixed into a mild filling", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe jamun (Indian blackberry), deseeded and mashed, when in season", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground hazelnut mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if hazelnut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a moong dal and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Is very low-TDS (RO) water actually a problem for your toddler?", "notes": "RO treatment commonly lowers dissolved minerals as well as unwanted contaminants. TDS alone does not establish whether water is healthy or unhealthy, and WHO does not set a health-based guideline value or universal minimum for TDS. Food is normally the principal source of calcium, magnesium and other essential minerals, so a low purifier reading should not by itself create alarm in a toddler eating a varied diet. The more important question is whether the treated water meets applicable microbial and chemical standards. Do not increase TDS by deliberately mixing untreated water back into drinking water unless the complete treatment system has been designed and verified to keep the final water safe.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with a squeeze of lime", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe muskmelon (kharbuja), deseeded and finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pistachio mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if pistachio has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Rice with a chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Fluoride: too little and too much both matter, and geography decides", "notes": "Fluoride is a case where both too little and too much matter, and where India specifically has documented regions with naturally high groundwater fluoride. The WHO''s guideline value is 1.5 mg/l; it explicitly names India, alongside China, Central Africa and South America, as a region with parts of the country where groundwater naturally contains high fluoride levels. Long-term excessive fluoride exposure during tooth development can cause dental fluorosis, while substantially higher and prolonged exposure can affect bones. Risk depends on the concentration, duration and total fluoride exposure, not on one water number in isolation. Boiling does not remove fluoride. If testing identifies excessive fluoride, use a source or treatment system specifically verified to reduce fluoride and confirm the plan through the relevant water authority or healthcare team. None of this tells you anything about your own water specifically — fluoride levels vary enormously by exact source, even within the same district — but it''s a genuine, geography-dependent reason to actually know your water''s fluoride level rather than assume either way, especially while your toddler''s permanent teeth are still developing underneath their baby teeth.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) uttapam with grated carrot and a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi (finger millet) uttapam with grated carrot and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe apricot (khubani), stone removed and finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with soft, seedless grapes, cut lengthwise into quarters", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Nitrate in groundwater, and how to actually check your own water", "notes": "Nitrate contamination can occur in groundwater affected by fertiliser, manure, wastewater or sewage. BIS specifies 45 mg/l as nitrate with no relaxation, while WHO uses a guideline value of 50 mg/l as nitrate. Young bottle-fed infants are especially vulnerable to nitrate-related methaemoglobinaemia, but this does not mean water above the applicable limit becomes acceptable once a child reaches toddlerhood. Water exceeding the standard should be treated as unsuitable for household drinking or food preparation until an appropriate authority identifies a safe source or a treatment method specifically verified to reduce nitrate. The Jal Jeevan Mission water-quality portal and Central Ground Water Board reports can provide useful area-level context, but they may not represent the water reaching a particular household or a private borewell. When the source is uncertain, arrange testing of the actual household water through a laboratory accredited for the required parameters. Ask the laboratory or local water authority which microbial and chemical tests are appropriate for that source. A purifier''s TDS display cannot replace contaminant-specific testing.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable semiya (vermicelli) upma with a squeeze of lime", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable semiya (vermicelli) upma with soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "Lobia (black-eyed peas), well mashed, mixed into a small bowl of plain curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Soft tender coconut (malai), finely chopped or mashed, mixed into a small bowl of plain curd", "dinner_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a masoor dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Bringing it together — knowing your water instead of guessing", "notes": "Putting this week together: boiling is an effective way to kill disease-causing germs when performed correctly, but it does not remove fluoride, nitrate or other dissolved chemicals. No purifier combination is universally ideal. RO, UV, UF, activated carbon and other technologies address different concerns, so the appropriate system depends on what testing or reliable local evidence shows is present. Check the unit''s verified contaminant-reduction claims and maintain it according to the manufacturer''s instructions. Store treated water in a clean, covered container and avoid dipping hands or used cups into it, because safe water can be recontaminated during storage.", "breakfast_a": "Soft dosa with a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Soft dosa with finely crumbled paneer mixed into a mild filling", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of plain curd with soft, ripe kiwi, peeled and finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground chia seed mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if chia has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a mixed dal and soft-cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Rice with a mixed dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Do you know where your household water comes from, which contaminants are relevant locally, and what your treatment method is verified to remove?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'A TDS number cannot answer the question parents actually care about. Understanding the water source, the locally relevant microbial and chemical concerns, and the verified purpose of the chosen treatment method provides a much clearer safety picture.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
