-- postpartum week 123: How much vitamin B6 does your toddler need, and where does it come from?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   123,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much vitamin B6 does your toddler need, and where does it come from?',
   '"Vitamin B6 helps your toddler''s body actually use the protein on their plate — and it''s already sitting in the dal, banana and potato you''re already serving."',
   'Vitamin B6 doesn''t get much attention, but it''s the coenzyme behind more than a hundred everyday reactions in your toddler''s body — most of them involved in putting protein to work: building muscle, making haemoglobin, and producing the brain chemicals behind mood and sleep. The amount a toddler needs is small, and vitamin B6 is distributed across many familiar foods, including pulses, potatoes, bananas, poultry and fish. A varied family diet will usually provide several sources across the week without requiring parents to count milligrams at each meal. Deficiency is uncommon in otherwise healthy toddlers eating varied meals, although medical conditions and certain medicines can alter risk; this week also covers the one supplement-safety nuance worth knowing accurately, and a brief note relevant to a specific medical situation, rather than either ignoring these questions or over-worrying about them. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely.',
   'If your toddler is being treated for tuberculosis, takes isoniazid, or is on any long-term medication: ask the treating doctor whether it affects vitamin B6 or other nutrient needs, and whether any supplement is part of the plan — this is a question for that conversation, not something to decide from a food list. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn every meal into detective work. A clear diagnosis, from the right professional, is what actually tells you which foods or medications are the concern. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— This week''s menu leans on chana and other pulses, banana, potato, chicken and fish — not because anything unusual is needed, but to make the point visible: an ordinary Indian family meal is already doing real vitamin B6 work. Wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully. ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "What vitamin B6 actually does, and how much your toddler needs", "notes": "Vitamin B6 works quietly behind the scenes: it''s a coenzyme in more than a hundred different reactions in the body, and most of them are about protein — helping build muscle and other tissue from the protein your toddler eats, and helping make haemoglobin, the part of red blood cells that carries oxygen. It also supports how the body makes some of the brain chemicals behind mood, sleep and alertness. The amount needed is small. The US NIH reference sets the recommended intake at 0.5 milligrams a day for children aged 1-3 years, while the ICMR-NIN reference used in India gives a somewhat higher figure for this age group. These values come from different reference frameworks, so they should not be treated as competing targets or combined into a new one. For an Indian family, the practical approach is to follow locally applicable guidance and offer a varied diet rather than calculate vitamin B6 meal by meal.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated carrot and a squeeze of lime", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated carrot and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe fig (anjeer), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground sesame (til) mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if sesame has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a masoor dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Chana and other pulses — an everyday vitamin B6 source", "notes": "If you wanted one everyday Indian food that quietly does a lot of vitamin B6 work, chana (chickpeas) would be a strong candidate — among the better plant sources available, alongside other pulses in smaller amounts. A toddler eats only a fraction of an adult portion, so the useful takeaway isn''t a precise number but a pattern: regularly including chana, rajma, or another pulse in your toddler''s meals — the same pulses already doing protein and folate work elsewhere in this series — contributes meaningfully toward vitamin B6 needs too, without requiring a different shopping list.", "breakfast_a": "Besan (gram flour) chilla with grated carrot and a smooth, mild mint chutney", "breakfast_b": "Besan (gram flour) chilla with grated carrot and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with a chana dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "Chana (chickpeas), well mashed, mixed into a small bowl of plain curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe sitaphal (custard apple), deseeded and mashed", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a rajma (kidney beans), well mashed, and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a rajma (kidney beans), well mashed, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Banana, potato, chicken and fish — other reliable everyday sources", "notes": "Beyond pulses, a handful of everyday foods carry real vitamin B6: banana, potato, chicken and fish all contribute a useful amount in an ordinary toddler portion. Fortified breakfast cereal is a major vitamin B6 source in some countries'' diets, but it isn''t a standard part of most Indian toddler breakfasts, so it''s not something to rely on here — the everyday combination of pulses, banana, potato and poultry or fish carries more of the weight in a typical Indian household. None of these need to appear every single day; the point is that vitamin B6 shows up across a wide, familiar range of foods rather than one single ingredient you have to remember.", "breakfast_a": "Aloo (potato) paratha, soft, with a small bowl of plain curd on the side", "breakfast_b": "Aloo (potato) paratha, soft, with finely crumbled paneer mixed into the filling", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked potato and peas, finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked potato, finely mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe banana, sliced", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pumpkin seed mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if pumpkin seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked potato and spinach (palak), finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Rice with a moong dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked potato, finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "What real deficiency looks like — and why it''s not a toddler-food concern", "notes": "True vitamin B6 deficiency is well understood partly because of a specific historical episode: in the 1950s, a batch of infant formula that had lost its vitamin B6 during processing caused irritability and seizures in affected infants before the cause was identified and corrected. In an older child or adult, deficiency can show up as a specific type of anaemia, skin changes around the mouth, and confusion. Vitamin B6 deficiency is uncommon in a healthy toddler eating a varied diet, but it can occur in association with certain medical conditions, malabsorption or medicines. These signs are not specific to vitamin B6 deficiency, so they should not be used for self-diagnosis. If a child has seizures, unusual weakness, persistent skin or mouth changes, pallor, poor growth or another concerning symptom, seek medical assessment rather than trying a vitamin supplement independently.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with grated carrot and a squeeze of lime", "breakfast_b": "Oats porridge with grated carrot and soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with a masoor dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with a masoor dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe papaya, deseeded and finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground roasted peanut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if peanut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Why ordinary food variety usually covers vitamin B6", "notes": "Vitamin B6 is involved in protein metabolism, but that does not mean protein intake and vitamin B6 intake are interchangeable. The reassuring part is that vitamin B6 occurs in many foods already used across a balanced family diet: pulses, potatoes and other starchy vegetables, bananas, poultry, fish and some fortified foods. Offering variety across these groups is more useful than trying to pair vitamin B6 with protein at every meal or track the nutrient separately.", "breakfast_a": "Jowar (sorghum) dosa, soft, with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Jowar (sorghum) dosa, soft, with a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with an arhar dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with an arhar dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe litchi, skin and seed removed, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe plum, stone removed and mashed", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "The one supplement caution, and a note for a specific medical situation", "notes": "High supplemental intakes of vitamin B6 can cause peripheral nerve damage, producing symptoms such as tingling, numbness, burning pain or problems with coordination. Recovery may occur after the excessive intake stops, but symptoms are not always quickly or completely reversible. Authorities have set different upper intake levels: the older US reference lists 30 milligrams a day for children aged 1-3 years, whereas EFSA''s 2023 assessment set a substantially lower level of 3.2 milligrams a day for that age group. These are population-level safety limits, not suggested supplement doses. The practical message is simpler: do not give a toddler a separate vitamin B6 supplement or combine multiple vitamin-containing products unless the child''s healthcare team has reviewed the products and doses. Isoniazid can interfere with vitamin B6 metabolism. Pyridoxine supplementation is recommended in some children receiving isoniazid, particularly children who are malnourished, living with HIV or otherwise considered at increased risk of neuropathy. The need and dose depend on the child''s treatment and clinical circumstances. If your toddler is receiving isoniazid for tuberculosis treatment or prevention, confirm with the treating doctor whether pyridoxine is included in the plan. Do not add it independently.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain uttapam with grated beetroot and a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Multigrain uttapam with grated beetroot and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe mulberries (shahtoot), finely mashed, when in season", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground sunflower seed mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if sunflower seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked ivy gourd (tindora), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a masoor dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked ivy gourd (tindora), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Bringing it together — one more nutrient your everyday plate already covers", "notes": "This week''s guidance is reassuring by design: if today''s meals included a pulse, some banana or potato, and a portion of chicken, fish or egg, they very likely contributed useful vitamin B6 — the same everyday foods this series has already been recommending for protein, folate and iron. There''s no special food combination to remember, and a separate supplement is not normally needed unless it has been recommended for a specific medical reason. The one thing worth carrying forward is the supplement caution: if a doctor ever prescribes a vitamin B6 or multivitamin supplement for your child — including, specifically, if your child is ever treated for tuberculosis — it''s worth asking how the dose fits into the bigger picture, rather than assuming more is simply better.", "breakfast_a": "Soft appam with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Soft appam with a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe mango, stone removed and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground roasted makhana (fox nut) mixed into a small bowl of warm milk", "dinner_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Rice with a chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Thinking back over this week''s meals, how many days already included a pulse, banana or potato, and some chicken, fish or egg — and did meeting that feel like extra effort?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'Vitamin B6 isn''t a nutrient that asks for anything extra — it asks you to notice what''s already there. Chana, banana, potato and a portion of chicken or fish can contribute useful vitamin B6 through ordinary family meals, without turning eating into a daily calculation.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 124: Building a balanced plate for your toddler: putting this series' food groups together
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   124,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'Building a balanced plate for your toddler: putting this series'' food groups together',
   '"You don''t need a new nutrient to think about this week — just a look at how the ones you already know fit together on one plate."',
   'Nobody eats one nutrient at a time; children eat meals made from different food groups. A useful toddler pattern includes variety across grains or millets, pulses and other protein foods, vegetables, fruit, dairy or appropriate alternatives, and energy-dense foods such as nuts, seeds and cooking fats. These groups do not have to appear in fixed proportions at every meal. What matters more is that they are offered repeatedly across the day and week in portions and textures appropriate for the child. A toddler''s appetite will naturally vary, so this is a flexible pattern for caregivers — not a plate the child must finish. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely.',
   'If your toddler has a diagnosed food allergy, coeliac disease, or another condition that limits which food groups they can eat from: the general proportions in this week''s guidance may need to be adapted around what''s actually safe for your child — this is a conversation for your paediatrician or dietitian, not something to work out from a general plate diagram. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn every meal into detective work. A clear diagnosis, from the right professional, is what actually tells you which foods or proportions are the concern. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— This week''s menu is the same kind of shared family meal this series always uses — the difference this week is in how you look at it. Notice, across the day, whether grains or millets, pulses or other protein foods, vegetables, fruit, dairy or suitable alternatives, and cooking fats were all offered somewhere along the way. Wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Using India''s food groups without shrinking an adult plate", "notes": "ICMR-NIN''s ''My Plate for the Day'' illustrates food-group variety for an approximately 2,000-calorie adult diet. Its energy figures describe how much energy the food groups contribute; they should not be read as the percentage of a toddler''s physical plate. ICMR-NIN provides separate guidance for children older than one year, whose energy needs, nutrient density, meal frequency and portion sizes differ from those of adults. The useful lesson to carry over is variety: offer grains or millets, pulses or another protein food, vegetables, fruit, dairy or a suitable alternative, and appropriate fats across the day. There is no need to reproduce one fixed diagram at every meal.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with a smooth, mild coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with a smooth, mild coconut chutney and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with a chana dal and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa) pulp, removed from the fibrous pod and mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with a chana dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa) pulp, removed from the fibrous pod and mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe guava, deseeded and finely mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground walnut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if walnut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a masoor dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Rice with a masoor dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Grains, millets and pulses: useful foundations, not the whole plate", "notes": "Rice, wheat and millets provide much of a young child''s everyday energy. Pulses such as dal, rajma and chana contribute protein, fibre and several micronutrients; eggs, fish, meat, tofu or other suitable foods can also contribute to the protein part of the day. A grain and a pulse often work well together, but they do not need to appear as a compulsory pair at every meal. The practical aim is to offer varied, nutrient-dense foods across the day without allowing one food group to crowd out vegetables, fruit or other important foods.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) dosa, soft, with grated bottle gourd (lauki) mixed through", "breakfast_b": "Ragi (finger millet) dosa, soft, with grated bottle gourd (lauki) mixed through and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa) pulp, removed from the fibrous pod and mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa) pulp, removed from the fibrous pod and mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe apple, peeled, cored and finely grated", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground peanut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if peanut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a moong dal and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a moong dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked carrot, finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Vegetables and fruit — colour as a useful shortcut", "notes": "Vegetables and fruit contribute vitamin C, carotenoids, folate, fibre and many other beneficial compounds. India''s adult reference plate assigns a substantial physical quantity to vegetables and fruit, even though their percentage contribution to total energy is lower than that of cereals or fats. This illustrates why energy percentages and visible plate space are not the same thing. For a toddler, offer vegetables and fruit regularly across the day in safe textures, using seasonal variety where possible. Colour can be a helpful prompt for variety, but no single colour rule guarantees that every nutrient requirement has been met.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with a smooth, mild tomato chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with a smooth, mild tomato chutney and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with a chana dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked pumpkin (kaddu), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe melon, deseeded and cut into small, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground hazelnut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if hazelnut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Dairy or alternatives, alongside varied protein foods", "notes": "Milk and curd can contribute protein, calcium and vitamin B12. Eggs, fish and meat contribute protein and several micronutrients, while pulses, tofu, nuts and seeds provide useful plant-based protein and other nutrients. These foods are not nutritionally identical: for example, ordinary unfortified plant foods do not provide reliable vitamin B12. A child who avoids dairy or all animal foods may need carefully selected fortified foods or supplements under appropriate guidance. There is no requirement to serve dairy and a separate animal-protein food at every meal; aim instead for suitable sources of protein, calcium, iron and vitamin B12 across the overall diet.", "breakfast_a": "Soft idli with grated beetroot mixed through", "breakfast_b": "Soft idli with grated beetroot mixed through and soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a masoor dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with a masoor dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe sweet lime (mosambi), peeled, with seeds and tough membranes removed, and cut into small pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pumpkin seed mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if pumpkin seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with a chana dal and soft-cooked okra (bhindi), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with a chana dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked okra (bhindi), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Fat supports growth, but \"unrestricted\" does not mean unlimited", "notes": "Young children need adequate dietary fat because it supplies concentrated energy and essential fatty acids and supports growth and development. ICMR-NIN''s child guidance lists approximately 37 g of total fat for children aged 1-3 years within a reference 1,110-kcal diet, although an individual child''s needs vary. Fat should come from a mixture of ordinary foods and cooking fats rather than mainly fried or highly processed foods. Do not impose an adult weight-loss diet on a toddler, but avoid describing fat as unlimited or suggesting that more ghee is automatically better. Follow the child''s growth pattern and any individual clinical advice.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain uttapam with a smooth, mild peanut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Multigrain uttapam with a smooth, mild peanut chutney and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal and soft-cooked okra (bhindi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with a mixed dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked okra (bhindi), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe pomegranate arils, thoroughly crushed or mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground sunflower seed mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if sunflower seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with a moong dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with a moong dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "What a realistic toddler plate looks like, meal to meal", "notes": "None of this needs to be assembled at every single meal for it to work. Think of the variety across a whole day, or even a couple of days, rather than a formula each plate must satisfy — a breakfast heavier in grains, a lunch that adds dal and a vegetable, a nourishment break that leans on fruit or dairy, and a dinner that rounds out protein. This is the same ''offer the food, let your child decide how much'' approach covered earlier in this series: your role is making sure the range of food groups is genuinely on offer across the day and week, not policing exactly what proportion ends up on the plate at any single sitting.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with a smooth, mild coriander chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a smooth, mild coriander chutney and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Rice with a moong dal and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with a moong dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe kiwi, peeled and finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground sesame mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if sesame has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with a mixed dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with a mixed dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Bringing it together — one shape, not a dozen separate rules", "notes": "This week''s picture is meant to simplify, not create another rule to track. Across the day and week, offer a changing combination of grains or millets, pulses and other protein foods, vegetables, fruit, dairy or suitable alternatives, and appropriate fats. Every meal does not need to contain every group, and a toddler does not need to clear a perfectly divided plate. The caregiver''s role is to provide regular, varied and developmentally appropriate choices; the child can decide whether and how much to eat from what is offered.", "breakfast_a": "Soft appam with grated cucumber and a squeeze of lime", "breakfast_b": "Soft appam with grated cucumber and a squeeze of lime, and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with an arhar dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with an arhar dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe plum, stone removed and finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground chia mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if chia has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with a masoor dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with a masoor dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Looking back over the week, were several different food groups offered regularly — and was your toddler allowed to respond to their own hunger and fullness rather than being expected to complete fixed portions?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'You''ve already been building a balanced plate for months, one shared family meal at a time — this week was just a chance to see the shape of what you''ve been doing all along.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 125: How much potassium does your toddler need, and where does it come from?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   125,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much potassium does your toddler need, and where does it come from?',
   '"Potassium is found across many familiar family foods — this week is about noticing those sources, not chasing a perfect number."',
   'Potassium is an electrolyte your toddler''s body uses to help regulate fluid balance, nerve signals, and muscle contraction — including the heart''s own rhythm. It''s spread naturally across foods this series'' menus already lean on: dal and other legumes, potato, curd, spinach, tomato, and a wide range of fruit. For most toddlers eating a varied diet, potassium isn''t something that needs separate planning or counting. What''s worth knowing this week is how differently reference authorities frame the exact number, and the specific, uncommon situations — a diagnosed kidney condition, or certain medicines — where general reassurance doesn''t apply and a paediatric team''s guidance should take over. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely.',
   'If your toddler has a diagnosed kidney condition, or takes a medicine that can affect blood potassium: follow your paediatric team''s individual guidance on potassium-containing foods this week, rather than the general reassurance given here — this is covered further on Day 5. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn every meal into detective work. A clear diagnosis, from the right professional, is what actually tells you which foods or proportions are the concern. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————- This week''s menu doesn''t change to specially feature potassium — it''s the same kind of shared family meal this series always uses. Notice, if you like, how often dal, potato, curd, tomato and fruit already appear without any special planning. Wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————-',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Potassium''s role: fluid balance, nerve signals, and your toddler''s heartbeat", "notes": "Potassium is an electrolyte — a mineral that carries an electrical charge in body fluids. Your toddler''s body uses it to help regulate fluid balance inside and outside cells, to transmit nerve signals, and to allow muscles to contract, including the heart''s own rhythm. It works closely alongside sodium, which this series covered in Week 31. None of this requires a toddler to think about electrolytes directly — it happens as a normal part of eating and drinking.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with mashed potato mixed through", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with mashed potato mixed through and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with arhar dal and soft-cooked sweet potato, finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with arhar dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked sweet potato, finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe orange segments, seeds and tough membranes removed, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground almond mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if almond has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and soft-cooked okra (bhindi), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked okra (bhindi), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Two reference numbers, one honest answer", "notes": "Different authorities express potassium guidance somewhat differently. ICMR-NIN''s 2020 report gives a recommended intake of approximately 1,750 mg a day for children aged 1-3 years, while the US National Academies'' Adequate Intake, also reported by the US NIH, is 2,000 mg a day for this age group. An Adequate Intake is an estimate based on observed healthy intakes, not a precisely measured minimum that parents need to reach every day. WHO advises adjusting its adult recommendation downward for children according to their energy needs and notes that the direct evidence in children is limited. These figures are useful reference points, not numbers families need to calculate at every meal. For most healthy toddlers, offering a varied pattern of legumes, vegetables, fruit, dairy or suitable alternatives is the more practical approach.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) dosa, soft, with a smooth, mild tomato-coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Ragi (finger millet) dosa, soft, with a smooth, mild tomato-coconut chutney and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with moong dal and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa) pulp, removed from the fibrous pod and mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with moong dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked drumstick (moringa) pulp, removed from the fibrous pod and mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe mango, stone removed and cut into small, soft, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground walnut mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if walnut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with arhar dal and soft-cooked tomato, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked tomato, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where potassium shows up in an ordinary Indian day", "notes": "Where potassium actually shows up is more useful than any single number: dal and other legumes, potato and sweet potato, curd and other dairy, spinach and other leafy vegetables, tomato, and a wide range of fruit — banana, orange, papaya, melon, apricot, and many others — all contribute meaningfully. These aren''t foods to specially add this week; they''re already staples of this series'' shared family menus, which is part of why potassium rarely needs deliberate attention in a varied diet.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated sweet potato mixed through", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated sweet potato mixed through and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with moong dal and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with moong dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe muskmelon, seeds and rind removed and cut into small, soft, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground cashew mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if cashew has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with masoor dal and thoroughly cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with masoor dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and thoroughly cooked amaranth (chaulai) leaves, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "No need to count it, but variety still matters", "notes": "For healthy people with normal kidney function, potassium from ordinary food is not known to cause harm, and experts have not set a Tolerable Upper Intake Level for dietary potassium because the available evidence was insufficient to establish one. Healthy kidneys normally remove excess potassium through urine. This does not make potassium supplements or potassium-containing salt substitutes suitable for a toddler without medical advice. For an otherwise healthy toddler eating a varied diet, there is no need to count milligrams or plan every meal around potassium; offering familiar foods from different food groups across the week is enough.", "breakfast_a": "Soft idli with finely chopped tomato mixed through, served with plain curd or sambar", "breakfast_b": "Soft idli with finely chopped tomato mixed through and soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with chana dal and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe chikoo (sapota), peeled and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pistachio mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if pistachio has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with moong dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with moong dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "The exception: kidney conditions and certain medicines", "notes": "Most toddlers do not need their potassium intake restricted. The important exception is a child whose healthcare team has identified a condition or medicine that affects blood-potassium levels — for example, impaired kidney function or certain medicines that reduce potassium excretion. In that situation, follow the child''s individual medical and dietary guidance rather than changing foods independently. Do not give a toddler a potassium supplement or potassium-containing salt substitute unless it has been specifically recommended for them.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain uttapam with mashed banana lightly folded through", "breakfast_b": "Multigrain uttapam with mashed banana lightly folded through and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with masoor dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with masoor dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe apricot, stone removed and cut into small, soft, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground peanut mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if peanut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with mixed dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with mixed dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "What an ordinary varied day can look like", "notes": "None of this needs conscious tracking to work. A day with dal or another legume, a potato or leafy vegetable, curd, and a piece of fruit somewhere across breakfast, lunch, the nourishment break and dinner already includes several foods that naturally provide potassium, without anyone having to think of it that way. This is the same theme running through this series: ordinary variety across a week does most of the work that a single ''superfood'' or supplement is often marketed as doing.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with a smooth, mild curry-leaf chutney", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable poha with a smooth, mild curry-leaf chutney and finely crumbled paneer mixed through", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with mixed dal and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with mixed dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe jamun, deseeded and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground sesame mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if sesame has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and soft-cooked potato, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with chana dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked potato, finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Bringing it together", "notes": "Potassium is a good example of a nutrient this series can mostly reassure you about rather than ask you to track: it does real work for fluid balance, nerves and muscles, including the heart, and it''s already spread across the dal, potato, curd, vegetables and fruit that make up an ordinary varied diet. The reference numbers differ by authority, and that''s fine to hold loosely. The one thing worth remembering precisely is the exception: a diagnosed kidney condition or a specific medicine, where individual paediatric guidance should take over from general reassurance.", "breakfast_a": "Soft appam with grated pumpkin mixed through, served with plain curd or a mild dal accompaniment", "breakfast_b": "Soft appam with grated pumpkin mixed through and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with mixed dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with mixed dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe mulberries (shahtoot), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground flaxseed mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if flaxseed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Looking back over this week''s meals, which everyday potassium-rich foods — dal, potato, curd, tomato, or fruit — already showed up regularly in your family''s meals, without anyone planning around them?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'You may not need to add a new food this week. Instead, notice which familiar sources of potassium already appear across your family''s meals. If your toddler eats only a very limited range of foods, or you''re concerned about growth or feeding, discuss the overall pattern with their healthcare team.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 126: How much vitamin K does your toddler need, and where does it come from?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   126,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much vitamin K does your toddler need, and where does it come from?',
   '"Vitamin K quietly supports normal blood clotting and bone health — and, for most healthy toddlers, ordinary varied meals provide familiar sources without needing separate tracking."',
   'Vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin your toddler''s body uses to activate the proteins involved in normal blood clotting and in building healthy bone. Most parents first hear about it at birth, when babies routinely receive vitamin K to protect against a bleeding risk in the newborn period — but that dose is preventive newborn care, not an ongoing supply. Now that your toddler eats family foods, leafy greens and other everyday foods contribute to their ongoing vitamin K intake, alongside a further contribution from gut bacteria, though how much the body actually absorbs and uses from that source remains uncertain. For most healthy toddlers eating a varied diet, this happens without any special planning. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely.',
   'If your toddler takes a blood-thinning medicine, or has a diagnosed condition affecting fat digestion or absorption: follow your paediatric team''s individual guidance on vitamin K and dietary fat this week, rather than the general reassurance given here — this is covered further on Day 5. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn every meal into detective work. A clear diagnosis, from the right professional, is what actually tells you which foods or proportions are the concern. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— This week''s menu doesn''t change to specially feature vitamin K — it''s the same kind of shared family meal this series always uses. Notice, if you like, how often leafy vegetables already appear in ordinary family meals. A modest amount of cooking fat can also help the body absorb vitamin K from those vegetables. Wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully. ——————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Vitamin K''s role: blood clotting and bone health", "notes": "Vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin, meaning it''s absorbed alongside dietary fat rather than dissolving in water. Your toddler''s body uses it to activate proteins involved in normal blood clotting — so a cut or scrape can stop bleeding as it should — and in bone metabolism, helping bind calcium into the proteins that build healthy bone. There are two main forms: vitamin K1 (phylloquinone), which comes mainly from green leafy vegetables and some plant oils, and vitamin K2 (menaquinones), found in smaller amounts in fermented and animal foods and also produced by bacteria living in the gut.", "breakfast_a": "Soft idli with finely mashed pumpkin mixed through, served with plain curd or sambar", "breakfast_b": "Soft idli with finely mashed pumpkin mixed through, served with plain curd or sambar and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with arhar dal and thoroughly cooked fenugreek (methi) leaves, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with arhar dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and thoroughly cooked fenugreek (methi) leaves, finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe persimmon, peeled and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground melon seed (magaz) mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if melon seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Two reference numbers, and a source that stays silent", "notes": "Reference bodies frame the amount somewhat differently. The US NIH''s Adequate Intake is 30 mcg a day for children aged 1-3 years, compared with 90 mcg for adult women and 120 mcg for adult men. WHO/FAO''s separate framework gives a lower Recommended Nutrient Intake of 15 mcg a day for the same age group, based on roughly 1 mcg per kilogram of body weight. Both are estimates built from observed healthy intakes rather than a precisely measured minimum, and ICMR-NIN does not publish a separate figure of its own for vitamin K. These numbers are reference points, not targets to calculate at every meal. For most healthy toddlers, regularly offering leafy vegetables as part of varied meals is the practical approach; families do not need to calculate vitamin K at every meal.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable oats porridge with grated carrot mixed through, served with plain curd or milk", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable oats porridge with grated carrot mixed through and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with mixed dal and thoroughly cooked mustard greens (sarson), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with mixed dal, a fully cooked egg curry and thoroughly cooked mustard greens (sarson), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe pineapple, core removed and finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely grated coconut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if coconut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with moong dal and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd), finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with moong dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd), finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "From the birth injection to your toddler''s everyday plate", "notes": "Many parents first hear about vitamin K when their baby receives vitamin K shortly after birth. Newborns begin life with very small vitamin K stores, only small amounts cross the placenta, and breast milk contains relatively little vitamin K. The birth dose protects babies against vitamin K deficiency bleeding during the vulnerable early months of life. It is preventive newborn care, not a substitute for the vitamin K children later obtain through food. Now that your toddler eats family foods, leafy vegetables, vegetable oils and other familiar foods contribute to their ongoing intake. Gut bacteria also produce forms of vitamin K, although how much the body absorbs and uses from this source remains uncertain.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with grated beetroot mixed through, served with plain curd or a mild dal accompaniment", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with grated beetroot mixed through and soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with mixed dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with mixed dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe dragon fruit, peeled and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground chironji (charoli) mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if chironji has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with arhar dal and thoroughly cooked fenugreek (methi) leaves, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and thoroughly cooked fenugreek (methi) leaves, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "No need to worry about vitamin K from ordinary food", "notes": "No Tolerable Upper Intake Level has been established for the naturally occurring forms of vitamin K because available evidence has not identified adverse effects from the amounts normally obtained from food. This is reassurance about ordinary meals, not a reason to give a toddler a vitamin K supplement. Supplements should be used only when specifically recommended for the child, particularly if they take medicine that interacts with vitamin K or have a condition affecting its absorption. There is no need to restrict leafy vegetables in an otherwise healthy toddler''s diet because of concern about ''too much'' vitamin K.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain uttapam with grated apple lightly folded through, served with plain curd", "breakfast_b": "Multigrain uttapam with grated apple lightly folded through and a small portion of mild, fully cooked minced mutton, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Rice with moong dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe strawberry, hulled and finely mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pine nut (chilgoza) mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if pine nut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with arhar dal and soft-cooked broccoli, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked broccoli, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "The exception: blood-thinning medicine and fat-absorption conditions", "notes": "General reassurance doesn''t apply to every child. Vitamin K intake needs individual medical guidance if a toddler takes an anticoagulant (blood-thinning) medicine that works by interfering with vitamin K, since changes in dietary intake can affect how well that medicine works — or if a toddler has a diagnosed condition affecting fat digestion or absorption, since vitamin K is fat-soluble and absorbed alongside dietary fat. Both situations are uncommon in an otherwise healthy toddler. If either applies to your child, follow their treating team''s specific dietary guidance rather than the general approach described this week.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable vermicelli upma with finely grated carrot mixed through, served with plain curd or a mild dal accompaniment", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable vermicelli upma with finely grated carrot mixed through and a small portion of fully cooked, lower-mercury fish, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and thoroughly cooked mustard greens (sarson), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and thoroughly cooked mustard greens (sarson), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, pitted dates, finely chopped and soaked until soft", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground cashew mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if cashew has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with chana dal and soft-cooked broccoli, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with chana dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked broccoli, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Where vitamin K shows up in an ordinary Indian day", "notes": "Leafy vegetables such as spinach (palak), fenugreek (methi), amaranth (chaulai) and mustard greens (sarson) are useful everyday sources of vitamin K. Some vegetable oils also provide vitamin K. Because vitamin K is fat-soluble, eating leafy vegetables as part of a meal containing a modest amount of cooking fat can help its absorption. Families do not need to create a special ''vitamin K meal'' or calculate how much each dish contains; offering varied vegetables across the week is the practical approach.", "breakfast_a": "Soft appam with mashed banana mixed through, served with plain curd or a mild dal accompaniment", "breakfast_b": "Soft appam with mashed banana mixed through, served with plain curd or a mild dal accompaniment and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with moong dal and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with moong dal, a fully cooked egg curry and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe pear, peeled, cored and cut into small, soft, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground flaxseed mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if flaxseed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with chana dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Bringing it together", "notes": "Vitamin K is a good example of a nutrient this series can mostly reassure you about: it does real, specific work in blood clotting and bone health. Vitamin K given after birth protects babies during the vulnerable early months of life, and from here on, ordinary meals containing leafy vegetables and some dietary fat can contribute meaningfully to your toddler''s vitamin K intake — already part of this series'' menus. The reference numbers differ by authority, and that''s fine to hold loosely. The one thing worth remembering precisely is the exception: a child on blood-thinning medicine or with a diagnosed fat-absorption condition, where individual medical guidance should take over from general reassurance.", "breakfast_a": "Soft besan (gram flour) chilla with finely grated bottle gourd mixed through", "breakfast_b": "Soft besan (gram flour) chilla with finely grated bottle gourd mixed through and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with masoor dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe watermelon, seeds removed and cut into small, soft, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground chia seed mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if chia seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with mixed dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "dinner_b": "Rice with mixed dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Looking back over this week''s meals, which familiar leafy vegetables already appeared in your family''s meals without anyone planning specifically around vitamin K?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'You may not need to add a new food this week. Instead, notice which familiar leafy greens and cooking oils already appear across your family''s meals. If your toddler eats only a very limited range of foods, or you''re concerned about growth or feeding, discuss the overall pattern with their healthcare team.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 127: When your toddler has ongoing loose stools: understanding "toddler's diarrhea" and what actually helps
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   127,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'When your toddler has ongoing loose stools: understanding "toddler''s diarrhea" and what actually helps',
   '"Some well-growing toddlers develop frequent loose stools without an underlying disease — but persistent diarrhea deserves a healthcare review before that reassuring pattern is assumed."',
   'Toddler''s diarrhea — frequent loose stools for weeks, in a child who is otherwise growing and thriving — is one of the most common reasons parents worry about their toddler''s digestion. But persistent loose stools can have several causes, so this diagnosis depends on a healthcare review first, not just on how well your toddler seems to be growing. Once a healthcare professional considers the pattern consistent with toddler''s diarrhea, the dietary picture is genuinely reassuring: a look at a few everyday patterns — fluid, fruit juice, fat and fibre — usually does more good than a search for a food to blame. This week walks through what the condition is, what''s worth reviewing with your doctor first, what''s worth adjusting once it''s confirmed, and the specific signs that call for prompt or urgent medical attention instead. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely.',
   'If your toddler shows signs of dehydration, repeated vomiting, severe abdominal pain, blood, pus or black stool, or loose stools that persist for weeks alongside poor growth, nighttime symptoms or a family history of coeliac disease or inflammatory bowel disease: seek medical care rather than following the general reassurance given here — the full range of urgent and less-urgent signs is covered on Day 5. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn every meal into detective work. A clear diagnosis, from the right professional, is what actually tells you which foods or proportions are the concern. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————– This week''s menu doesn''t change to specially treat loose stools — it''s the same kind of shared family meal this series always uses, already free of the sweet drinks and juice this week is cautious about. Whole fruit usually doesn''t need to be eliminated: offer an age-appropriate portion rather than unlimited fruit, and change a specific fruit only if your child''s healthcare team identifies it as contributing to symptoms. Wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully. ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————–',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "What healthcare professionals mean by toddler''s diarrhea", "notes": "Toddler''s diarrhea, also called functional diarrhea or chronic nonspecific diarrhea of childhood, describes frequent loose or watery stools in a young child who otherwise appears well and continues to grow normally. It commonly begins during the toddler or preschool years and usually improves as the digestive system matures. However, persistent diarrhea can have several causes, and normal growth alone does not confirm the diagnosis. If loose stools continue for several weeks, discuss the pattern with your child''s healthcare team before assuming it is toddler''s diarrhea or making significant dietary changes.", "breakfast_a": "Soft moong dal cheela with finely grated carrot mixed through", "breakfast_b": "Soft moong dal cheela with finely grated carrot mixed through and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft vegetable daliya with moong dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft vegetable daliya with moong dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe banana, mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground fox nut (makhana) mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if fox nut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with arhar dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "The everyday patterns healthcare teams may review", "notes": "Once other causes have been considered, healthcare professionals may review several dietary patterns sometimes remembered as the ''4 F''s'': excess fluid — particularly juice and sweet drinks — fruit juice or other poorly absorbed sugars, insufficient dietary fat, and an unsuitable amount of fibre. This does not mean restricting the water a thirsty child needs. Continue to offer water normally, and never deliberately reduce essential fluids during diarrhea. If your toddler can''t drink enough, is vomiting repeatedly, or shows signs of dehydration, seek medical advice promptly rather than trying to manage the problem through food changes alone.", "breakfast_a": "Soft besan (gram flour) chilla with finely grated carrot mixed through", "breakfast_b": "Soft besan (gram flour) chilla with finely grated carrot mixed through and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe orange segments, seeds and tough membranes removed, mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground black sesame (kala til) mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if black sesame has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "dinner_b": "Rice with masoor dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Fruit juice and why it matters more than people expect", "notes": "Fruit juice — apple and pear juice especially — is one of the most common everyday contributors. These juices contain more fructose than glucose, and the body absorbs fructose less efficiently when it isn''t balanced by glucose; the unabsorbed sugar draws water into the gut and is fermented by gut bacteria, producing loose, watery stools. The US AAP recommends no juice at all before 12 months of age, and at most 4 ounces a day for a toddler aged 1-3 years — and is explicit that juice has no role in treating diarrhea or dehydration, despite sometimes being offered for exactly that reason. Whole fruit is the better everyday choice regardless of this week''s topic.", "breakfast_a": "Multigrain uttapam with grated carrot lightly folded through, served with plain curd or sambar", "breakfast_b": "Multigrain uttapam with grated carrot lightly folded through and soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with mixed dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with mixed dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe papaya, deseeded and cut into small, soft, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pecan mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if pecan has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with chana dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Dietary fat: avoid an unnecessarily low-fat pattern", "notes": "Dietary fat can slow the movement of food through the intestine, so an unnecessarily low-fat diet may contribute to loose, fast-moving stools in some children with confirmed toddler''s diarrhea. The practical response is to make sure your toddler''s overall diet contains an age-appropriate amount of fat from familiar foods such as curd, paneer, eggs, nut or seed pastes and the cooking fat already used in family meals. Don''t automatically change the type or quantity of milk, add large amounts of ghee, or deliberately create a high-fat diet. If you''re unsure what''s appropriate for your child''s age, growth or medical history, ask their healthcare team.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated pumpkin mixed through", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated pumpkin mixed through and a small portion of mild, fully cooked minced mutton, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with chana dal and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with chana dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe muskmelon, deseeded and finely mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pistachio mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if pistachio has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with masoor dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked French beans, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When this isn''t toddler''s diarrhea: red flags, and how urgently to act", "notes": "Toddler''s diarrhea is a diagnosis that depends on everything else being ordinary, and it''s confirmed by your child''s healthcare team rather than assumed at home. Two different levels of concern call for two different responses. Seek prompt or urgent medical care if you notice: signs of dehydration (reduced urination or noticeably fewer wet nappies, a dry mouth, no tears when crying, sunken eyes, or unusual thirst), unusual sleepiness, weakness or reduced responsiveness, repeated vomiting or an inability to keep fluids down, severe or worsening abdominal pain, or blood, pus, or black, tarry stool. Arrange a routine medical evaluation — rather than assuming toddler''s diarrhea — if loose stools persist for several weeks, growth slows or weight gain stalls, symptoms wake your toddler at night, stools are unusually greasy or foul-smelling, there''s a family history of coeliac disease or inflammatory bowel disease, or the pattern follows recent foreign travel, exposure to unsafe water, or a prolonged course of antibiotics.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable oats porridge with grated pumpkin mixed through, served with plain curd or milk", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable oats porridge with grated pumpkin mixed through and a small portion of fully cooked, lower-mercury fish, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with arhar dal and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and lightly steamed spinach (palak), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe papaya, deseeded and finely mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground hazelnut mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if hazelnut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with mixed dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with mixed dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "No elimination diets, and where fibre actually fits", "notes": "Once the pattern has been assessed as toddler''s diarrhea, routinely removing foods or ordering broad food-allergy panels is usually unnecessary — the condition isn''t driven by a specific food your toddler is reacting to, so removing foods one at a time rarely helps and can create new gaps in an already-narrow toddler diet. A clinician may recommend targeted testing or a supervised dietary trial when the history suggests a different cause, such as a specific food allergy or coeliac disease. Fibre guidance is genuinely mixed across paediatric sources: the practical approach is to keep fibre at an ordinary, age-appropriate level from fruit, vegetables and wholegrains, rather than either maximising or restricting it. A day with ordinary dairy, a modest amount of whole fruit, and fibre from ordinary meals — without any extreme change in a single direction — is what most paediatric guidance actually describes.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with grated carrot mixed through, served with plain curd or a mild dal accompaniment", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable upma with grated carrot mixed through and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked knol khol (kohlrabi), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe banana, cut into small, soft, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground almond mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if almond has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft paratha with mixed dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "dinner_b": "Soft paratha with mixed dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Bringing it together", "notes": "Once a healthcare professional considers the pattern consistent with toddler''s diarrhea, this week''s approach is genuinely reassuring: frequent loose stools in a toddler who is otherwise growing and thriving are usually explained by ordinary dietary patterns rather than illness. Reducing excess juice and sweet drinks while continuing normal access to water, and making sure the diet isn''t unnecessarily low in fat, are the adjustments most likely to help — not an elimination diet or a hunt for a trigger food. What''s worth remembering precisely is the range of signs that call for prompt or urgent medical attention instead of this week''s general reassurance — covered in full on Day 5.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable vermicelli upma with finely grated beetroot mixed through, served with plain curd or a mild dal accompaniment", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable vermicelli upma with finely grated beetroot mixed through and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft khichdi with arhar dal and soft-cooked pumpkin, finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft khichdi with arhar dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked pumpkin, finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe orange segments, seeds and tough membranes removed, finely diced", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground watermelon seed mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if watermelon seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Rice with moong dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Looking back over this week, does your toddler''s routine already lean on a lot of juice or other sweet drinks, or on reduced-fat dairy — and is there room to quietly bring fat back up and juice down, without turning it into a project?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'You likely don''t need to change your toddler''s diet dramatically this week. Instead, notice how much juice or other sweet drinks your toddler has day to day, and whether their meals include enough everyday fat. If loose stools continue alongside any of this week''s red flags, or you''re simply unsure, bring the pattern to their healthcare team rather than guessing. If your child''s loose stools have continued for several weeks, arrange a healthcare review even if they seem otherwise well and are growing normally.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
