-- postpartum week 128: How much vitamin E does your toddler need, and where does it come from?
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   128,
   NULL,
   'Phase 5 of 6 — Beyond the first year',
   'How much vitamin E does your toddler need, and where does it come from?',
   '"Vitamin E quietly protects your toddler''s cells as an antioxidant — and, for most healthy toddlers, suitable cooking oils, nuts, seeds and green vegetables can provide it through ordinary family meals without separate tracking."',
   'Vitamin E is a fat-soluble vitamin your toddler''s body uses mainly as an antioxidant, protecting the fatty membranes around cells from everyday chemical damage. Unlike some nutrients in this series, vitamin E rarely makes headlines. Vitamin E deficiency is very rare in otherwise healthy children. When it occurs, it is usually associated with a condition that affects fat digestion, absorption or vitamin E metabolism rather than an ordinary varied diet. Food sources include certain cooking oils — particularly sunflower and safflower oils — along with nuts, seeds and green vegetables. Many families already use some of these foods as part of ordinary meals, and for most healthy toddlers eating a varied diet, this happens without any special planning. Using this shared menu These meals are designed so the family can begin with the same basic food. Serve the mother a satisfying adult portion, including enough protein, vegetables and energy for her own needs. Adapt only the toddler''s portion to their chewing ability: remove bones and hard seeds, reduce salt and chilli, and cut or soften food as needed. A toddler does not need every family food mashed if they can already manage ordinary family textures safely.',
   'If your toddler has a diagnosed condition affecting fat digestion or absorption, or you''re considering a vitamin E supplement for them: follow your paediatric team''s individual guidance on vitamin E this week, rather than the general reassurance given here — this is covered further on Day 5. If you manage diabetes, PCOS or insulin resistance: keep your prescribed meal, monitoring and medication schedule steady, and pair rice, roti, fruit or toast with protein or fibre where practical. If you manage high blood pressure: keep added salt, pickles, papad and packaged sauces within the limits advised for you. If you take thyroid medication: continue it exactly as prescribed and maintain any required separation from iron or calcium supplements. If you are being treated for anaemia: continue prescribed iron, and follow the timing instructions provided for taking it around tea, coffee, calcium or thyroid medication. None of this needs to turn every meal into detective work. A clear diagnosis, from the right professional, is what actually tells you which foods or proportions are the concern. Your seven-day shared family menu Choose either option at each meal according to your family''s preferences, available ingredients and dietary pattern. A and B are alternatives; you do not need to prepare both. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————– This week''s menu doesn''t change to specially feature vitamin E — it''s the same kind of shared family meal this series always uses. Depending on the oil your family uses, cooking oil may contribute vitamin E; nuts, seeds and green vegetables provide additional food sources. Do not offer whole nuts to children under five. Offer nuts and larger or hard seeds finely ground, crushed to an appropriate texture, incorporated into food, or as a thinly spread smooth butter. Wash all produce thoroughly with reliably safe water, and cook ingredients fully. —————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————–',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Vitamin E''s role: protecting cells as an antioxidant", "notes": "Vitamin E is a fat-soluble vitamin that acts mainly as an antioxidant, protecting the fatty membranes surrounding your toddler''s cells from a kind of chemical damage called oxidative stress. It occurs naturally in eight related forms — four tocopherols and four tocotrienols — but alpha-tocopherol is the form the body maintains and uses preferentially, and the one reference intakes are based on. Because it''s fat-soluble, vitamin E is absorbed alongside dietary fat rather than dissolving in water, much like vitamins A, D and K.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha with grated carrot mixed through, served with plain curd", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable paratha with grated carrot mixed through and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with arhar dal and soft-cooked pumpkin, finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked pumpkin, finely mashed", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe guava, peeled and mashed", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground sunflower seed mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if sunflower seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with moong dal and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked green beans, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "How much vitamin E does your toddler need?", "notes": "The US NIH recommends 6 mg of vitamin E a day, measured as alpha-tocopherol, for children aged 1-3 years. Other authorities do not always provide the same kind of age-specific target: the WHO/FAO review found insufficient evidence to establish a recommended intake for age groups beyond infancy. These differences reflect limitations in the available evidence, not a need for parents to calculate vitamin E at every meal. For most healthy toddlers, regularly offering a varied diet that includes suitable oils, nuts or seeds and vegetables is the practical approach.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla with finely grated spinach mixed through, served with plain curd", "breakfast_b": "Moong dal chilla with finely grated spinach mixed through and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with masoor dal and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked cauliflower, finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe banana, cut into small, soft, coin-shaped pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground walnut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if walnut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with chana dal and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked bottle gourd (lauki), finely mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where vitamin E comes from in food", "notes": "Vitamin E is concentrated in vegetable oils, nuts and seeds, and leafy vegetables. Sunflower oil and safflower oil are useful everyday sources for an Indian kitchen, and sunflower seeds, almonds and peanuts add more. Do not offer whole nuts to children under five. Offer nuts and larger or hard seeds finely ground, crushed to an appropriate texture, incorporated into food, or as a thinly spread smooth butter. Spinach and other leafy greens contribute a smaller amount. Because vitamin E is fat-soluble, pairing these foods with a meal that already contains some dietary fat helps the body absorb it — one more reason this series doesn''t ask families to remove all cooking fat from a toddler''s diet.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) porridge with mashed banana mixed through, served with milk", "breakfast_b": "Ragi (finger millet) porridge with mashed banana mixed through and a small portion of mild, fully cooked minced mutton, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Rice with mixed dal and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Rice with mixed dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked ridge gourd (turai), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe papaya, deseeded and finely chopped", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground pumpkin seed mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if pumpkin seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with arhar dal and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with arhar dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and soft-cooked cabbage, finely shredded If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why vitamin E deficiency from diet alone is rare", "notes": "Vitamin E deficiency is very rare in otherwise healthy children. When it occurs, it is usually associated with a condition that affects fat digestion, absorption or vitamin E metabolism rather than an ordinary varied diet — such as cystic fibrosis, a liver condition affecting bile flow, or certain other digestive conditions. This is a reassuring pattern this series has met before with other fat-soluble vitamins: ordinary food is rarely the problem; an underlying condition affecting fat digestion, absorption or metabolism is what actually causes deficiency.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable idli with finely mashed carrot mixed through, served with plain curd or sambar", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable idli with finely mashed carrot mixed through and a small portion of fully cooked, lower-mercury fish, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with moong dal and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked bhindi (okra), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, seedless grapes, quartered lengthwise", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground peanut mixed into a small bowl of warm milk, if peanut has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked broccoli, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Rice with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked broccoli, finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "The exception: fat-absorption conditions and vitamin E supplements", "notes": "General reassurance doesn''t apply to every child. A toddler with a diagnosed condition affecting fat digestion or absorption — such as cystic fibrosis or a liver condition affecting bile flow — needs individual guidance from their treating team on vitamin E, since the same mechanism that makes ordinary food safe also means their body may not absorb it normally. Do not give a toddler a vitamin E supplement unless their healthcare professional recommends it. High-dose vitamin E supplements may increase bleeding risk and can interact with medicines that affect blood clotting. This concern relates to supplements, not to the vitamin E naturally present in ordinary foods.", "breakfast_a": "Wheat dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated apple mixed through, served with milk", "breakfast_b": "Wheat dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated apple mixed through and soft scrambled egg (bhurji), lightly spiced and fully cooked, on the side", "lunch_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd), finely mashed", "lunch_b": "Soft roti with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked tinda (apple gourd), finely mashed, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe orange segments, seeds and tough membranes removed, cut into small, soft, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground black sesame (kala til) mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if black sesame has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Rice with arhar dal and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed", "dinner_b": "Rice with arhar dal, a fully cooked, lower-mercury fish curry, finely flaked and carefully checked for bones, and soft-cooked beetroot, finely mashed If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Where vitamin E shows up in an ordinary Indian day", "notes": "Sunflower oil and safflower oil are particularly useful vitamin E sources in an Indian kitchen, alongside almonds, peanuts and sunflower seeds offered ground or as a thinly spread nut butter. Leafy greens like spinach add a further contribution. None of this needs a special ''vitamin E meal.'' Because the type and quantity of cooking oil are not specified in this menu, it should not be assumed that the oil alone provides a particular amount. The ground nuts, seeds and green vegetables offered across the week provide additional sources without requiring parents to calculate a daily total.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam with finely chopped tomato mixed through, served with a mild dal accompaniment", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable uttapam with finely chopped tomato mixed through and a small portion of mild, fully cooked chicken keema, finely crumbled", "lunch_a": "Soft paratha with mixed dal and thoroughly cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "lunch_b": "Soft paratha with mixed dal, a tender, finely shredded mutton curry, fully cooked and free of bone fragments, and thoroughly cooked spinach (palak), finely chopped", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe guava, peeled and cut into small, soft, manageable pieces", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground almond, lightly roasted, mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if almond has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft khichdi with moong dal and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft khichdi with moong dal, boneless, finely shredded chicken and soft-cooked snake gourd (chichinda), finely chopped If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food.", "still_hungry": null}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Bringing it together", "notes": "Vitamin E is another example of a nutrient this series can mostly reassure you about: it plays a real, specific role protecting your toddler''s cells. Depending on the oil your family uses, cooking oil may contribute vitamin E; nuts, seeds and green vegetables provide additional food sources, already part of this series'' menus. Reference guidance differs between authorities because evidence for precise age-specific requirements remains limited. Parents do not need to resolve these differences or calculate a daily total when their toddler is eating a varied diet. The one thing worth remembering precisely is the exception: a toddler with a diagnosed fat-absorption condition, or any consideration of a vitamin E supplement, is where individual medical guidance should take over from general reassurance.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated beetroot mixed through, served with milk", "breakfast_b": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) porridge with grated beetroot mixed through and a fully cooked hard-boiled egg, finely chopped, on the side", "lunch_a": "Rice with masoor dal and soft-cooked carrot (gajar), finely grated", "lunch_b": "Rice with masoor dal, a fully cooked egg curry and soft-cooked carrot (gajar), finely grated", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with soft, ripe fig (anjeer), quartered", "nourishment_break_b": "Finely ground watermelon seed mixed into a small bowl of plain curd, if watermelon seed has already been introduced and tolerated", "dinner_a": "Soft roti with chana dal and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped", "dinner_b": "Soft roti with chana dal, soft tofu, finely crumbled, and soft-cooked capsicum, finely chopped, if soy has already been introduced and tolerated If either of you is still hungry: Offer more of the meal, or choose curd, milk, fruit with a protein-rich accompaniment, or another suitable family food. Looking back over this week''s meals, which everyday cooking oils, nuts or seeds did your family already use, without anyone thinking of them as a source of vitamin E?", "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'You may not need to add a new food this week. Instead, notice which everyday cooking oils, nuts, seeds and leafy greens already appear across your family''s meals. If your toddler eats only a very limited range of foods, or you''re concerned about growth or feeding, discuss the overall pattern with their healthcare team.',
   'Next week continues Phase 5 with another part of life beyond the first year, carrying forward this week''s approach: a few well-evidenced adjustments, and permission to let go of the rest.')
